\h Markos
\toc1 Markos
\toc2 Markos
\toc3 Mrk
\mt2 The account of Yeshua’s ministry by
\mt1 Markos
\is1 Introduction
\is2 Author
\ip This account about Yeshua’s activities and teachings was written by Markos (typically shortened to ‘Mark’ in English), the son of a Jewish family who lived in Yerushalem/Jerusalem. His mother was named Maria/Mary (Acts 12:12).
\ip Many people think he was Yohan/John Markos, a cousin of Barnabas (Col. 4:10) who accompanied Paul on his first long-distance trip to spread the good message about Yeshua the messiah (Acts 12:25, 13:13). We also know that Markos accompanied Peter (1 Peter 5:13), and some consider that it might have been Peter who narrated to Markos what Yeshua had done and taught.
\is2 This account
\ip This account seems to have been written for non-Jews, especially perhaps those familiar with Roman customs. So he didn’t use as much ink as Matthew or Luke explaining prophecies from the Jewish scriptures but he does however, take time to explain Jewish customs to help non-Jewish readers.
\ip Markos explains here that Yeshua came to serve both God and the people, and he often refers to him as ‘humanity’s child’ (traditionally translated very literally from the Greek as the ‘Son of Man’). We can see that especially in 10:45: “Even humanity’s child didn’t come to be served, but to serve others and to give his life as a ransom to set many people free.” Markos wants his readers to know that Yeshua did incredible things, but especially focuses on Yeshua’s teaching. Markos confirms Yeshua’s power and authority by telling about the miracles that he did, his healing of the sick, and his exorcism of demons.
\ip The ending of this account is often disputed, and scholars are still \jmp debating|link-href="https://textandcanon.org/a-case-for-the-longer-ending-of-mark"\jmp* about whether or not the longer ending (16:9-20) is original to Markos. The \em OET\em* includes the disputed longer ending, but displays it in a lighter colour to indicate its debatable status.
\iot Main components of Markos’ account
\io1 Preparation for and arrival of the messiah \ior 1:1-13\ior*
\io1 Yeshua’s activities in and around Galilee \ior 1:14–9:50\ior*
\io1 The transition from Galilee to Yerushalem \ior 10:1-52\ior*
\io1 The final week in Yerushalem \ior 11:1–15:47\ior*
\io1 Yeshua comes back to life \ior 16:1-20\ior*
\rem This is still an early look into the unpolished text of the Open English Translation of the Bible. Please double-check the text in advance before using in public.
\ie
\c 1
\m
\v 1 \add Here\add* begins the good message \add about\add*\f + \fr 1:1 \ft As well as ‘about’, the Greek construction can also have the connotation of ‘brought by’, or even imply both. (\xt Yohan 1:1 \ft also confirms that Yeshua was both the messenger and the message itself.)\f* God’s son, \nd Yeshua\nd* \add the\add* \nd messiah\nd*:
\s1 The ministry of Yohan the immerser (The return of ‘Eliyah’)
\r (Mat. 3:1-12; Luke 3:1-18; Yhn. 1:19-28)
\p
\v 2 The prophet Yeshayah wrote:\x + \xo 1:2: \xt Mal 3:1.\x*
\q1 “Look, I’m sending my messenger \add ahead of\add* you,
\q1 \add the one\add* who’ll be preparing your path.
\q1
\v 3 The voice \add out\add* in the wilderness shouting,\x + \xo 1:3: \xt Isa 40:3 (LXX).\x*
\q2 ‘Get the path ready for \nd Yahweh\nd*—
\q2 make it smooth for him \add to walk on\add*.’ ”
\p
\v 4 Yohan \add (John)\add* \add turned up\add* in the wilderness announcing that people should get immersed \add in water\add* \add in order to show that\add* their sins \add have been forgiven\add*,
\v 5 and \add many\add* from the Yudean region and from Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add* city went out to \add hear\add* him, and \add asked Yohan\add* to immerse them in the Yarden \add (Jordan)\add* river as they confessed their sins.
\p
\v 6 Yohan was dressed in \add simple clothes made from\add* camel hair with a leather belt around his waist, and he \add lived on\add* locusts and wild honey.\x + \xo 1:6: \xt 2Ki 1:8.\x*
\v 7 He \add also\add* announced, “There’s a greater \add man\add* coming \add soon\add*—\add in fact\add* I’m not \add even good enough\add* to bend down and untie his sandal \add straps\add*.
\v 8 I immersed you all in water, but he will immerse you in \add God’s\add* holy \nd spirit\nd*.”
\s1 Yeshua gets immersed and then tempted
\r (Mat. 3:13–4:11; Luke 3:21-22; 4:1-13)
\p
\v 9 \fig Yeshua was immersed by Yohan.|src="images/41_Mk_01_06_RG.jpg" size="col" loc="1:9" copy="© Sweet Publishing" ref="1:9"\fig*\add Then one day\add* Yeshua came from \nd Nazareth\nd* in the Galilee \add region\add*, and he got immersed in the Yarden \add river\add* by Yohan.
\v 10 \add As\add* Yeshua came up out of the water, he saw the sky being \add opened up\add*, and \add God’s\add* \nd spirit\nd* coming down on him like a dove.
\v 11 And a voice came from the sky \add saying\add*, “You are my son who I love \add and\add* \add am pleased with\add*.”\x + \xo 1:11: \xt Gen 22:2; Psa 2:7; Isa 42:1; Mat 3:17; 12:18; Mrk 9:7; Luk 3:22.\x*
\p
\v 12 \add After that,\add* the \nd spirit\nd* sent him \add out\add* into the wilderness
\v 13 \add where\add* he was tested by Satan for forty days. He was \add out there\add* with the wild animals, but messengers\f + \fr 1:13 \ft The Greek doesn’t actually distinguish between human messengers and heavenly messengers (so-called ‘angels’—the Greek word just transliterated letter-by-letter instead of being actually translated).\f* \add looked after\add* him \add there\add*.
\s1 The selection of the first followers/apprentices
\r (Mat. 4:12-22; Luke 4:14-15; 5:1-11)
\p
\v 14 After Yohan had been \add arrested\add*, \nd Yeshua\nd* went back up \add north\add* to Galilee, announcing the good news from \nd God\nd*
\v 15 saying, “\wj The \+add promised time is here\+add*, and God’s kingdom is \+add now\+add* near. \+add Turn from your sins\+add* and believe this good news.\wj*”\x + \xo 1:15: \xt Mat 3:2.\x*
\p
\v 16 As he went around Lake Galilee, \add Yeshua\add* saw \add two\add* fishermen, Simon and \add his\add* brother Andrew, throwing a net into the lake
\v 17 and \add he\add* said to them, “\wj Come \+add with\+add* me and I’ll make you into fishers of people.\wj*”
\v 18 \add So\add* they left their \add fishing work\add* and \add went off with\add* \add Yeshua\add*.
\p
\v 19 Then a bit further on he saw \add two\add* of Zebedee’s sons, Yacob and his brother Yohan, in a boat getting the nets ready.
\v 20 \add Yeshua\add* called them and they \add too\add* \add went off with\add* him, leaving their father Zebedee in the boat with the hired workers.
\s1 Yeshua drives out a demon
\r (Luke 4:31-37)
\p
\v 21 They \add went on\add* into Capernaum \add where\add* as soon as a rest day started, he would go into the \add Jewish meeting hall\add* \add (Grk: synagogue)\add* \add and\add* teach \add there\add*.
\v 22 \add The congregation\add* was amazed at his teaching, because he taught like \add someone\add* with authority, not like the \add regular teachers\add* \add there\add*.\x + \xo 1:22: \xt Mat 7:28-29.\x*
\p
\v 23 Then a \add demon-possessed\add* man in the \add meeting hall\add* suddenly yelled out,
\v 24 “What are we to you, \nd Yeshua\nd* of \nd Nazareth\nd*? You’ve come to destroy us. I know that you’re God’s holy one.”
\p
\v 25 But \nd Yeshua\nd* scolded \add it\add*,\f + \fr 1:25 \ft The Greek pronoun is masculine, implying that the man was the one being scolded, yet “Come out of him!” implies that the spirit is being spoken to.\f* saying, “\wj Keep quiet!\wj*”, and, “\wj Come out of him!\wj*”
\p
\v 26 Then the demon \add threw the man into a convulsion\add*, cried out \add loudly\add*, and \add left\add* him.
\v 27 Everyone was astounded \add and asked each other\add*, “What \add just happened\add*? \add We haven’t heard anything like this before—he has authority to command demons, and they obey\add* him!”
\v 28 \add So then\add* the news about \add Yeshua\add* spread all over \add that entire\add* Galilee region.
\s1 Yeshua heals many others
\r (Mat. 8:14-17; Luke 4:38-41)
\p
\v 29 After leaving the \add meeting hall\add*, Yeshua went with Yacob and Yohan into the home of Simon and Andrew.
\v 30 \add Inside,\add* Simon’s mother-in-law was lying down \add with a fever\add*, and \add the others\add* spoke to \add Yeshua\add* about her.
\v 31 When \add he went up to her, and\add* took hold of her hand \add and\add* \add helped her up\add*, the fever left her and she \add prepared something for them to eat\add*.
\p
\v 32 \add That\add* evening when the sun set, \add the people\add* brought all those \add who were sick or\add* demon-possessed to \add Yeshua\add*.
\v 33 \add It seemed like\add* the \add entire\add* town had gathered there \add outside\add* the \add front\add* door.
\v 34 \add Yeshua\add* healed many \add who were sick from a range of\add* diseases, and he \add commanded many demons to leave\add*—not allowing \add them\add* to speak because they knew that he was the \nd messiah\nd*.
\s1 Yeshua prays before going preaching
\r (Luke 4:42-44)
\p
\v 35 In the \add small hours of\add* the morning, \add Yeshua\add* got up and went out to a quiet place and prayed there.
\v 36 \add But\add* Simon and the others \add came\add* looking for him,
\v 37 and \add when\add* they found him they said, “Everyone’s looking for you!”
\p
\v 38 “\wj \+add Well,\+add*\wj*” Yeshua answered, “\wj we’ll be going into the \+add other villages around here\+add*, so that I can also tell \+add them\+add*. \+add That’s what I came here for\+add*.\wj*”
\v 39 Then he went preaching in all their \add meeting halls\add* in Galilee, and \add driving\add* out demons.\x + \xo 1:39: \xt Mat 4:23; 9:35.\x*
\s1 Yeshua heals a man with leprosy
\r (Mat. 8:1-4; Luke 5:12-16)
\p
\v 40 \add One day,\add* a \add man with leprosy\add* came to \add Yeshua\add* asking him, “\add I know that you could heal me if you wanted to\add*.”
\p
\v 41 \add Yeshua\add* felt compassion \add for him\add*, \add reached\add* out his hand and touched \add him\add*, and said, “\wj \+add Yes, I will. Get well\+add*.\wj*”
\v 42 Then immediately the leprosy \add disappeared\add* and he was \add well again\add*.
\v 43 \add So\add* \add Yeshua\add* sent him \add away\add*, \add but\add* warned him:
\v 44 “\wj Don’t talk to anyone—\+add just\+add* go to the priest and show him \+add that you’re well\+add*, and as a testimony of that, make the offering that Mosheh commanded \+add to be declared clean again\+add*.\wj*”\x + \xo 1:44: \xt Lev 14:1-32.\x*
\p
\v 45 But \add after the man left\add*, he started telling \add lots of people\add* and spreading the news—\add so much\add* so that \add Yeshua\add* could no longer come into the town but had to stay out in \add quieter\add* places, and \add people\add* \add kept\add* coming to him.
\c 2
\s1 Yeshua heals a paralysed man
\r (Mat. 9:1-8; Luke 5:17-26)
\p
\v 1 \add When\add* \add Yeshua\add* came \add back\add* to Capernaum \add after a few\add* days, \add people soon\add* heard that he was \add back\add* in the house.
\v 2 And \add so\add* many people \add turned up there\add* that there wasn’t any room left—not even in the doorway. \add Inside,\add* \add Yeshua\add* was \add explaining the scriptures\add* to them.
\v 3 Four \add men arrived, carrying a paralysed man\add* to \add Yeshua\add*,
\v 4 \add but\add* because of the crowd, \add they were unable to get close\add*. \add So\add* they \add climbed up onto the flat\add* roof, \add removed a section\add*, and lowered the \add paralysed man\add* lying on a pallet.
\v 5 Yeshua, observing their faith, said to the \add man\add*, “\wj Son, your sins are forgiven!\wj*”\f + \fr 2:5 \ft Yeshua’s hearers would have taken this as a claim to be God, as they knew that only God could forgive sins as v7 shows.\f*
\p
\v 6 But some of the religious teachers were sitting there, \add thinking to themselves\add*,
\v 7 “Why’s that fellow talking like that? He’s \add seriously insulting\add* God. \add There’s only\add* one who can forgive sins, \add and that’s\add* God \add himself\add*.”
\p
\v 8 Then \nd Yeshua\nd*, knowing in his spirit that they were reasoning like that, said to them, “\wj Why are you \+add thinking that to yourselves\+add*?\wj*
\v 9 \wj Which is easier: to tell the \+add paralysed man\+add* that his sins have been forgiven, or to tell him to pick up his \+add bedding\+add* and walk?\wj*
\v 10 \wj But so you all can see that humanity’s child \+em does\+em* have authority here on the earth to forgive sins,\wj*” he \add turned and\add* said to the \add paralysed man\add*,
\v 11 “\wj \+add Listen\+add*. Get up, take your \+add bedding\+add*, and go home!\wj*”
\p
\v 12 \add So\add* \add the man\add* stood up, and picked up \add his bedding\add*, and went out \add of the house where everyone saw him\add* and marvelled and \add praised\add* \nd God\nd* saying, “We’ve never seen \add anything like that\add* \add happen before\add*!”
\s1 Levi’s (Matthew’s) calling
\r (Mat. 9:9-13; Luke 5:27-32)
\p
\v 13 \add Yeshua\add* went \add back\add* out beside the lake where \add crowds of people\add* kept coming to him and he taught them.
\v 14 As he was passing by the tax office, he saw Levi Alfaeus sitting \add there\add* and said to him, “\add Come along with\add* me.” \add So\add* Levi stood up and followed \add Yeshua\add*.
\p
\v 15 \add Yeshua\add* \add ended up having a meal\add* at \add Levi’s\add* \add place\add*, and many \add other\add* tax collectors and \add undesirables\add* were sitting \add there also\add*, along with \nd Yeshua\nd* and his followers, because \add by then\add*, many \add people\add* were following him \add around\add*.
\v 16 \add When\add* the teachers from the Pharisee party \add noticed\add* that \add Yeshua\add* was eating \add beside undesirables\add* and tax-collectors, they \add asked\add* his followers, “\add How come\add* he eats with tax-collectors and \add other low-life\add*?”
\p
\v 17 \nd Yeshua\nd* \add overheard\add* them and said, “\wj Healthy people don’t need a doctor—it’s the sick ones that do. I didn’t come \+add here\+add* to \+add help those who think they have no needs\+add*, but \+add I came\+add* to call sinners.\wj*”
\s1 The question of fasting
\r (Mat. 9:14-17; Luke 5:33-39)
\p
\v 18 \add Now\add* the followers of Yohan-the-immerser, and \add members of\add* the Pharisee party, \add regularly\add* fasted. \add So\add* \add a small group\add* came to \add Yeshua\add* and asked, “\add How come\add* Yohan’s followers and the Pharisee party fast \add regularly\add*, yet your followers don’t?”
\p
\v 19 And \nd Yeshua\nd* \add answered\add*, “\wj Can the \+add groomsmen\+add* fast when they’re \+add celebrating\+add* along with him? \+add No,\+add* as long as \+add he’s\+add* still there, they \+add won’t be\+add* fasting.\wj*
\v 20 \wj But \+add in the future\+add*, the groom will be taken away from them, and \+em then\+em* they’ll be fasting.\wj*
\p
\v 21 “\wj No one sews a patch of \+add new\+add*, unshrunken cloth onto \+add used clothes\+add*, because the \+add new\+add* patch will tear away from that old fabric, and it will \+add end up with\+add* a worse tear.\wj*
\v 22 \wj And no one puts new wine into old wineskin \+add containers\+add*, because the wine will burst the \+add already stretched\+add* wineskins, and then both the wine and the wineskins will be destroyed. New wine must be put into \+em new\+em* wineskins.\wj*”
\s1 Working on the Rest Day
\r (Mat. 12:1-8; Luke 6:1-5)
\p
\v 23 \add Then it so happened that\add* \add Yeshua\add* and his followers passed through some fields of grain on some days of rest. \add As they went through\add*, they plucked off some heads of grain,\x + \xo 2:23: \xt Deu 23:25.\x*
\v 24 and \add some members of\add* the Pharisee party said, “\add Hey, it’s breaking the law to harvest grain\add* on a rest day!”
\p
\v 25 “\wj You’ve read \+add yourselves\+add*,\wj*” \add Yeshua\add* \add replied\add*, “\wj what David did when he and his men were hungry.\wj*
\v 26 \wj \+add They\+add* went into God’s \+add tent\+add* and ate the \+add sacred\+add* bread \+add representing God’s\+add* presence. Only the priests \+add were allowed to eat that\+add*, and yet \+add David\+add* even gave some to his \+add companions\+add*!\wj*”\x + \xo 2:26: \xt Lev 24:9.\x*
\v 27 Then he told the Pharisees, “\wj The \+add days\+add* of rest \+add were created for people\+add*, not \+add people\+add* for the rest \+add days\+add*.\wj*
\v 28 \wj So \+add I\+add*, humanity’s child, \+add have authority also over\+add* the day of rest.\wj*”
\c 3
\s1 The man with the curled-up hand
\r (Mat. 12:9-14; Luke 6:6-11)
\p
\v 1 Again \add at another time\add*, Yeshua went into the \add Jewish meeting hall\add* and there was a man there with a curled-up hand.
\v 2 The \add religious leaders there\add* were watching him closely \add to see\add* if he would heal the man on the rest day, \add and then if he did, they would have a case\add* against him.
\v 3 Then \add Yeshua\add* said to the man with the flawed hand, “\wj \+add Stand up there\+add* in the middle.\wj*”
\v 4 And he asked \add the leaders\add*, “\wj Is it allowable to do good on the rest \+add day\+add*, or \+add only\+add* to do evil? To save life, or to kill?\wj*” But they \add didn’t reply\add*.
\p
\v 5 After \add angrily\add* looking around at them, grieving at \add their complete lack of compassion\add*, he told the man, “\wj \+add Open up\+add* your hand!\wj*” The man stretched out \add his fingers\add*, and his hand \add became totally normal\add*.
\v 6 \add But\add* the Pharisees \add quickly left\add*, and \add meeting\add* with King Herod’s supporters, they \add conspired to kill\add* \add Yeshua\add*.
\s1 Big crowds press against Yeshua
\p
\v 7 \add After that,\add* \nd Yeshua\nd* withdrew towards the lake with his followers \add as well as\add* a huge crowd \add of people\add* from the \add regions of both\add* Galilee and \add down in\add* Yudea,
\v 8 \add along with others\add* from Yerushalem, Idumea, from across the Yordan \add River\add*, and from \add up\add* near Tsor \add (Tyre)\add* and Tsidon \add (Sidon)\add*. That huge crowd came \add because\add* they had heard about the \add amazing\add* things he had done.
\v 9 \add So\add* \add Yeshua\add* asked his apprentices \add to organise\add* a small boat to be there for him so he wouldn’t be cramped by \add the press of\add* the crowd.\x + \xo 3:9-10: \xt Mrk 4:1; Luk 5:1-3.\x*
\v 10 \add Virtually anyone who had any physical issues\add* was pressing towards him to touch him, because he had \add already\add* healed so many.
\v 11 And the \add demons\add*, whenever they \add saw\add* \add Yeshua\add*, they fell \add to their knees on\add* the ground in front of him and yelled out, “You are God’s son!”
\v 12 \add However\add* he \add regularly\add* warned them not to \add reveal who he was\add*.
\s1 Yeshua appoints his twelve representatives
\r (Mat. 10:1-4; Luke 6:12-16)
\p
\v 13 As \add Yeshua\add* \add started\add* going up a hill, he called the followers \add that he had selected\add* and they went off with him.
\v 14 He appointed twelve \add of them\add* as his representatives so that they could \add stick\add* with him, and be sent off to be proclaiming \add his message\add*
\v 15 and to also have authority to exorcise demons.
\p
\v 16 \add These are\add* the twelve that he appointed: Simon (but he appended the name “Peter”),
\v 17 Yacob \add (James)\add* and his brother Yohan \add (John)\add* who were Zebedee’s sons (and he nicknamed them the “sons of thunder”),
\v 18 Andrew, Philip, Bartholomew, Matthew, Thomas, Yacob Alfaeus, Thaddeus, Simon \add from\add* Canaan,
\v 19 and Yudas \add (Judas)\add* Iscariot who \add later\add* betrayed him.
\s1 The two opposing spiritual powers
\r (Mat. 12:22-32; Luke 11:14-23; 12:10)
\p
\v 20 Then \add Yeshua\add* was approaching a house, but the crowd \add swarmed them\add* again so they \add had no opportunity\add* to eat.
\v 21 The ones with him heard \add all the noise\add* and came to get him, because they were \add wondering if he was crazy\add*.
\p
\v 22 The religious teachers had come \add up\add* from Yerushalem and were \add complaining\add*, “\add He’s possessed by the prince of demons\add*. He \add can command\add* \add them\add* because \add he rules\add* \add them\add*!”\x + \xo 3:22: \xt Mat 9:34; 10:25.\x*
\p
\v 23 \add But\add* \add Yeshua\add* \add addressed\add* them: “\wj How could Satan \+add command\+add* \+add himself\+add*?\wj*
\v 24 \wj If a kingdom is divided against itself, that kingdom \+add could never prosper\+add*.\wj*
\v 25 \wj If a \+add household\+add* is divided against itself, that \+add family will never prosper\+add*.\wj*
\v 26 \wj \+add So\+add* if Satan \+add attacked\+add* himself and was split, he \+add couldn’t possibly be powerful\+add*, but \+add his time would be over\+add*.\wj*
\p
\v 27 \wj \+add On the contrary\+add*, no one can enter a strong man’s house to \+add clean out his possessions\+add* unless he first ties up that strong man—\+add after that he can work on the goods\+add*.\wj*
\p
\v 28 \wj \+add Honestly, I assure\+add* you that any sins of a \+add person\+add* can be forgiven, \+add no matter how much they curse\+add* \+add God\+add*.\wj*
\v 29 \wj But anyone who \+add speaks against\+add* the Holy Spirit can \+add never\+add* be forgiven, but is eternally \+add responsible\+add* for \+add their\+add* sin.\wj*”\x + \xo 3:29: \xt Luk 12:10.\x*
\v 30 (\add He said that\add* because they were saying that he was \add demon-possessed\add*.)
\s1 Yeshua’s mother and brothers arrive
\r (Mat. 12:46-50; Luke 8:19-21)
\p
\v 31 \add Now\add* \add Yeshua’s\add* mother and brothers came and were standing outside, and they \add asked someone to tell him that they were there\add*.
\v 32 \add Some in\add* the crowd sitting around him told \add Yeshua\add* that his mother and brothers were \add waiting outside for him\add*.
\p
\v 33 \add But\add* \add Yeshua\add* answered, “\wj Who is my mother and \+add who are\+add* my brothers?\wj*”
\v 34 And having looked around at the \add people\add* sitting here, he said, “\wj \+add Actually, you all are\+add* my mother and my brothers,\wj*
\v 35 \wj because anyone who \+add obeys\+add* \+nd God\+nd*, that \+add person\+add* is my brother and my sister and my mother.\wj*”
\c 4
\s1 The parable about planting seed
\r (Mat. 13:1-9; Luke 8:4-8)
\p
\v 1 Again \add Yeshua\add* started teaching \add by the edge of\add* the lake. A huge crowd gathered \add around\add* him, \add but\add* he had boarded a boat that was \add floating in the water\add*, and all the people were \add there\add* on the \add shore\add*.\x + \xo 4:1: \xt Luk 5:1-3.\x*
\v 2 \add From there\add*, he taught them many \add different\add* things in parables, \add including this one\add*:
\v 3 “\wj Listen. \+add Once there was a farmer\+add* who went to \+add spread some seed\+add*.\wj*
\v 4 \wj \+add As he spread it\+add*, some landed on the path, and the birds came and \+add fed on\+add* it.\wj*
\v 5 \wj \+add Some\+add* landed on \+add a rocky part without a lot of\+add* soil, and it \+add quickly sprouted\+add* in the \+add warm\+add*, \+add shallow\+add* soil,\wj*
\v 6 \wj \+add but\+add* when the sun came up, it \+add dried out\+add* because \+add the roots weren’t deep\+add*, \+add and so\+add* it \+add just\+add* withered up.\wj*
\v 7 \wj \+add Some of the seed landed in a thistle patch\+add*, and the thistles \+add grew\+add* and choked it out, \+add so the plants never bore a crop\+add*.\wj*
\v 8 \wj \+add But some\+add* \+add seed\+add* landed in \+add fertile\+add* soil and it \+add bore a harvest\+add*—\+add some of\+add* it \+add multiplying\+add* thirty \+add times\+add*, some sixty, and some a hundred \+add times\+add*.\wj*”
\p
\v 9 Then \add Yeshua\add* \add added\add*, “\wj \+add Anyone who wants to understand\+add*, let \+add them\+add* \+add consider it\+add*.\wj*”
\s1 Why Yeshua speaks in parables
\r (Mat. 13:10-17; Luke 8:9-10)
\p
\v 10 \add Later\add* when \add the crowd had left\add*, the twelve \add apprentices\add* asked him about the parables
\v 11 and he told them, “\wj You’ve \+add been given understanding\+add* about God’s kingdom, but \+add for others\+add*, \+add they’ll get it\+add* in parables\wj*
\q1
\v 12 \wj so that \+add when they look\+add*, they might see \+add but\+add* not perceive,\wj*
\q1 \wj and \+add when they listen\+add*, they might hear \+add but\+add* not understand,\wj*
\q1 \wj otherwise they might turn \+add from their sins\+add* and \+add be\+add* forgiven.\wj*”\x + \xo 4:12: \xt Isa 6:9-10 (LXX).\x*
\s1 The ground where the seed fell
\r (Mat. 13:18-23; Luke 8:11-15)
\p
\v 13 Then \add Yeshua\add* said to them, “\wj \+add If you didn’t understand\+add* this parable, how will you \+add understand any others\+add*?\wj*
\v 14 \wj The \+add person\+add* \+add spreading\+add* seed is \+add spreading\+add* the \+add good\+add* message,\wj*
\v 15 \wj and \+add there are some\+add* along the path where the \+add good\+add* message is being \+add spread\+add*, who hear, \+add but\+add* Satan \+add turns up\+add* immediately and \+add snatches\+add* the message that had been \+add planted\+add* in them.\wj*
\v 16 \wj Similarly, there’s \+add some seed\+add* that falls onto rocky places where \+add the people\+add* who hear the \+add good\+add* message \+add happily accept\+add* it,\wj*
\v 17 \wj \+add but\+add* don’t \+add make strong roots\+add*—\+add just\+add* temporary \+add changes\+add*. \+add So when they get mocked or attacked about accepting\+add* the \+add good\+add* message, they \+add quickly\+add* stumble.\wj*
\v 18 \wj Others are the ones \+add where the seed was spread\+add* among the thistles. They heard the \+add good message\+add*,\wj*
\v 19 \wj \+add but contemporary topics\+add*, \+add or the lure of money\+add*, or the appetite for other things, choke \+add their good intentions\+add* and \+add the message\+add* becomes unfruitful \+add in them\+add*.\wj*
\v 20 \wj \+add But\+add* \+add lastly\+add*, \+add the seed\+add* that landed on the good soil \+add represents\+add* \+add the people\+add* who hear the \+add good\+add* message, accept it, and are \+add fruitful\+add* thirty, sixty, or \+add even\+add* a hundred times \+add over\+add*.\wj*”
\s1 The light will shine on everything
\r (Luke 8:16-18)
\p
\v 21 Then \add Yeshua\add* told them, “\wj \+add Obviously no one goes and gets a\+add* lamp, \+add and then\+add* puts it under a basket or under the bed, \+add but rather they place\+add* it \+add somewhere higher\+add* on a stand.\wj*\x + \xo 4:21: \xt Mat 5:15; Luk 11:33.\x*
\v 22 \wj \+add You see, nothing\+add* is hidden \+add that won’t\+add* \+add eventually\+add* be disclosed, and there’s no secret that won’t \+add eventually\+add* \+add be revealed\+add*.\wj*\x + \xo 4:22: \xt Mat 10:26; Luk 12:2.\x*
\v 23 \wj Anyone who \+add wants to understand must listen carefully\+add*,\wj*”
\v 24 and he continued, “\wj \+add Take care what you listen to\+add*. \+add How much you all judge others, so too will judgement be handed out\+add* to you, and \+add even more\+add*.\wj*\x + \xo 4:24: \xt Mat 7:2; Luk 6:38.\x* DOUBLE-CHECK
\v 25 \wj Anyone who has \+add faith\+add* will be given \+add a reward\+add*, \+add but if you have none\+add*, even \+add the little\+add* you have will be taken away.\wj*”\x + \xo 4:25: \xt Mat 13:12; 25:29; Luk 19:26.\x* DOUBLE-CHECK
\s1 The parable about the sprouting seed
\p
\v 26 And then, “\wj God’s kingdom is like a \+add person\+add* throwing \+add some\+add* seed onto the ground.\wj*
\v 27 \wj \+add Whether\+add* \+add the person\+add* sleeps \+add at night\+add* or \+add stays up\+add* night and day, the seed will sprout and grow \+add either way\+add*, and \+add they\+add* don’t know how \+add it does it\+add*.\wj*
\v 28 \wj The earth is \+add designed\+add* \+add to multiply the seed\+add*: first a \+add sprout\+add* \+add appears\+add*, then a \+add bigger plant\+add*, and then the full head of grain.\wj*
\v 29 \wj \+add Then when it’s ripe\+add*, \+add the harvester\+add* is sent in because \+add it’s ready for\+add* harvest.\wj*”\x + \xo 4:29: \xt Yoel 3:13.\x*
\s1 The small seed produces a large result
\r (Mat. 13:31-32,34; Luke 13:18-19)
\p
\v 30 \add Again\add* he said, “\wj What could we liken God’s kingdom to? Or what parable could we \+add explain it with\+add*?\wj*
\v 31 \wj It’s like \+add planting\+add* a mustard seed in the ground—\+add a very small seed\+add*.\wj*
\v 32 \wj \+add But\+add* when it’s \+add planted and grows up\+add*, it \+add forms a large bush with many\+add* branches so that birds can nest in its \+add shade\+add*.\wj*”
\p
\v 33 And \add so as much as they could handle it\add*, \add Yeshua\add* \add shared\add* the \add good\add* message with them \add via many similar word pictures\add*.
\v 34 He \add never spoke without those illustrations\add*, but he explained all of them to his \add close\add* followers.
\s1 Yeshua halts the storm on the lake
\r (Mat. 8:23-27; Luke 8:22-25)
\p
\v 35 \add It was already\add* becoming evening on that day, \add so\add* \add Yeshua\add* told \add his apprentices\add*, “\wj Let’s \+add all\+add* \+add go across to the other shore\+add*.\wj*”
\v 36 Then after sending the crowd away, they got into the boat with him, plus there were \add some\add* other boats with \add them\add*.
\v 37 \add Out on the lake,\add* a big storm \add came up with strong winds\add*, and the waves started breaking \add across\add* the boat and filling it \add with water\add*.
\v 38 \add However,\add* Yeshua \add was asleep\add* on a cushion in the back, \add so\add* they woke him, \add calling out\add*, “Teacher, don’t you care that we’re \add all about to drown\add*?”
\p
\v 39 \add Now awake\add*, \add Yeshua\add* scolded the wind and \add waves\add*, “\wj Quieten down! Stop that!\wj*” Then the wind died down and \add the lake\add* became very calm.
\v 40 \add Turning back to them\add* he \add asked\add*, “\wj Why were you all afraid? Don’t you have \+add any\+add* faith yet?\wj*”
\p
\v 41 \add But now\add* they were \add very\add* afraid \add again\add*, and \add asked\add* each other, “Who could this \add man\add* be that both the wind and the \add waves obey\add* him?”
\c 5
\s1 Yeshua sends the demons into pigs
\r (Mat. 8:28-34; Luke 8:26-39)
\p
\v 1 \add So\add* they got \add safely\add* to the Gerasenes region on the \add other\add* side of the lake.
\v 2 As \add Yeshua\add* \add climbed\add* out of the boat, a \add demon-possessed\add* man \add came\add* out of the tombs to meet him.
\v 3 He \add lived\add* in \add those\add* tombs, and no one could tie him up, not even with chains,
\v 4 because he had often been tied up \add in the past\add* with shackles and chains but had always pulled the chains apart and \add smashed\add* the shackles, and no one was able to subdue him.
\v 5 All through the day and night, \add whether\add* in the tombs or \add up\add* in the hills, he was yelling out and \add hurting\add* himself with stones.
\p
\v 6 \add So\add* when he had seen \nd Yeshua\nd* in the distance, he ran and \add as a sign of honour\add* fell to \add his knees on the ground\add* in front of him.
\v 7 Then he called out \add very loudly\add*, “What \add will happen between us\add*, \nd Yeshua\nd*—the son of the highest God? I beg you in God’s \add name\add* not to torment me.”
\v 8 Because \add Yeshua\add* had \add already started\add* saying, “\wj Come out of the man you \+add ungodly\+add* spirit!\wj*”
\p
\v 9 \add Yeshua\add* asked, “\wj What’s your name?\wj*”
\p The man \add answered\add*, “My name is ‘Thousands’ because there’s many of us,”
\v 10 and he begged \add Yeshua\add* not to send them out of the country.
\p
\v 11 \add It just so happened that\add* there was a large herd of pigs being fed near the hill,
\v 12 and \add the demons\add* begged \add Yeshua\add* \add again\add* saying, “Send us to the pigs so that we can enter them.”
\v 13 \add Yeshua\add* \add agreed to that\add*, \add so\add* the \add ungodly\add* spirits came out \add of the man\add*, went into the pigs, and the \add entire\add* herd of some two thousand \add pigs\add* rushed down the steep bank into the lake and were drowned.
\p
\v 14 The ones who had been feeding them \add rushed off\add* and \add spread the news around\add* in the city and the \add surrounding area, So\add* \add the people\add* \add hurried\add* out to see what had happened.
\v 15 \add When they arrived where\add* \nd Yeshua\nd* was, they saw the man who had been demon-possessed, \add fully-\add*clothed and \add sitting there in full control of his senses\add*. \add They knew it was\add* the \add man\add* called ‘Thousands’ and they \add became frightened\add*.
\v 16 Those who had seen it all happen described it \add all\add* to \add the people\add*—\add what had happened\add* \add both\add* to the demon-possessed \add man\add* and to the pigs.
\p
\v 17 \add So\add* then the people \add began\add* asking \add Yeshua\add* to go away from their region.
\p
\v 18 \add As\add* \add Yeshua\add* was \add climbing back\add* into the boat, the \add man\add* who had been demon-possessed \add wanted\add* to go with him.
\v 19 \add But\add* \add Yeshua\add* wouldn’t let him and told him \add to head back\add* home to his own people, and \add explain\add* to them what the \nd master\nd* had done for him, and \add how\add* he had been merciful to him.
\p
\v 20 \add So\add* he went away and \add shared with\add* \add the people in\add* the Ten Cities region what \nd Yeshua\nd* had done for him, and they were all amazed.
\s1 A woman healed as Yeshua goes to help a dying girl
\r (Mat. 9:18-26; Luke 8:40-56)
\p
\v 21 \add When\add* \nd Yeshua\nd* had crossed in the boat \add to the other side\add* \add of the lake\add*, a large crowd \add of people\add* \add came out to the lake edge to see\add* him.
\v 22 Then \add unexpectedly\add*, one of the \add religious\add* leaders named Yairos \add (Jairus)\add* came out, and \add when he found\add* \add Yeshua\add*, he fell \add to his knees in front of him\add*
\v 23 and pleaded, “My \add young\add* daughter is \add critical and\add* \add just hanging on\add* for you to come and \add touch\add* her so that she’ll be healed and live.”
\p
\v 24 \add So\add* \add Yeshua\add* went off with \add Yairos\add*. The large crowd followed \add also\add*, and \add many were getting pressed against\add* \add Yeshua\add*.
\p
\v 25 Then a woman \add came who\add* \add had been losing\add* blood for twelve years,
\v 26 and who’d endured much under \add various\add* doctors \add despite spending everything she had\add*. \add After all that, she’d ended up even\add* worse,
\v 27 \add but\add* when she heard about what Yeshua \add had been doing\add*, \add she worked her way through\add* the crowd \add to the back of\add* \add Yeshua and\add* touched his \add clothes\add*,
\v 28 because she had told \add herself\add*, “If I could even just touch his clothes, I’d be healed.”
\p
\v 29 Immediately the \add bleeding stopped\add*, and she knew that she had been healed.
\v 30 Immediately \add also\add*, \nd Yeshua\nd* knew that power had gone out of him, \add and\add* he turned back towards the crowd \add and\add* said, “\wj Who touched my clothes?\wj*”
\p
\v 31 \add But\add* his followers said, “You can see \add all the people\add* that were pressing on you \add so why would you ask\add* who touched \add you\add*?”
\p
\v 32 \add But\add* \add Yeshua\add* was \add still\add* looking around to see \add who\add* had done it
\v 33 \add when\add* the woman, \add scared and shaking because of what was happening\add*, came and fell \add to her knees\add* in front of \add Yeshua\add* and told him the \add whole story\add*.
\v 34 \add Yeshua\add* said, “\wj Daughter, your faith has healed you. Go in peace and \+add full health\+add*.\wj*”
\p
\v 35 While he was still speaking, \add messengers\add* came from \add the religious leader’s\add* house to tell \add Yairos\add*, “Your daughter has died. Why bother the teacher any more?”
\p
\v 36 But \nd Yeshua\nd* overheard them telling \add that\add* to the leader and told \add him\add*, “\wj Don’t be afraid—just keep believing.\wj*”
\v 37 Yeshua didn’t allow anyone to accompany him other than Peter, and Yacob \add (James)\add* and his brother, Yohan \add (John)\add*.
\v 38 As they approached \add Yairos’\add* house, \add Yeshua\add* saw a \add big\add* commotion \add ahead of them\add*, with lots of wailing and crying \add going on\add*.
\v 39 As he entered, he said, “\wj Why are you all crying \+add in distress\+add*? The child hasn’t died—she’s \+add just\+add* sleeping!\wj*”
\p
\v 40 \add But\add* they \add just\add* ridiculed \add Yeshua\add*, \add so\add* he \add sent them all outside\add*, \add just\add* taking the father and mother of the child, \add along with three\add* with him. He went in where the \add girl\add* was,
\v 41 and taking \add her\add* hand, he said, “\wj Young woman, I tell you: get up!\wj*”
\v 42 Immediately \add she\add* got up and walked around, and \add everyone\add* marvelled \add at the amazing thing that had happened\add*. (She was twelve years old.)
\v 43 \add Yeshua\add* \add repeatedly\add* told them \add to keep this to themselves\add*, and \add added\add* that \add they\add* should give her \add something\add* to eat.
\c 6
\s1 Yeshua’s community can’t believe he’s from God
\r (Mat. 13:53-58; Luke 4:16-30)
\p
\v 1 \add So\add* \add Yeshua\add* \add left\add* \add Capernaum\add* and \add went back with his followers\add* to \add Nazareth,\add* his \add birthplace\add*.
\v 2 \add It was now\add* the Rest Day and when he started teaching in the \add Jewish meeting hall\add*, many who heard him were astonished, saying, “Where did that man learn all that? \add How could he have such\add* wisdom and \add be able to do those\add* miracles?
\v 3 Isn’t that the carpenter, the son of Maria \add (Mary)\add*, and the brother of Yacob and Yosef CHECK and Yudas and Simon? \add Don’t his sisters live around\add* here?” And they were offended by his \add teaching\add*.
\p
\v 4 Then \nd Yeshua\nd* told them, “\wj A prophet is \+add only dishonoured\+add* in his \+add own\+add* home town, and among his \+add own\+add* relatives, and in his \+add own\+add* home.\wj*”\x + \xo 6:4: \xt Yhn 4:44.\x*
\p
\v 5 He wasn’t able to do \add even\add* one miracle there, except for \add placing\add* his hands on a few sick \add people\add* and healing them.
\v 6 He \add was amazed at how they wouldn’t believe him as\add* he went around the villages teaching.
\s1 Yeshua sends his apprentices out in pairs
\r (Mat. 10:5-15; Luke 9:1-6)
\p
\v 7 Then he called his twelve \add apprentices\add* and started sending them out \add in pairs\add* and giving them authority over \add demons\add*.
\v 8 He \add insisted\add* that they \add can carry\add* a walking stick, but not to take \add food\add*, a backpack, or money.
\v 9 \add They could wear one pair of\add* sandals, \add but not take changes of\add* clothes,
\v 10 and he told them, “\wj Whenever you’re \+add invited\+add* into a home, stay \+add at that one place\+add* until you all \+add leave that town\+add*.\wj*
\v 11 \wj \+add But if any places don’t want you or don’t want to hear what you have to say\+add*, \+add just\+add* \+add leave that place\+add*. \+add As you go,\+add* shake the dust off your \+add sandals so they can see\+add* \+add that you pass the responsibility of your leaving, onto them\+add*.\wj*”\x + \xo 6:11: \xt Acts 13:51.\x*
\p
\v 12 \add So\add* \add the apprentices\add* went off and \add started\add* telling \add people\add* to repent.
\v 13 \add In addition,\add* they \add drove\add* out many demons, and anointed many sick people with olive oil and healed them.\x + \xo 6:13: \xt Jam 5:14.\x*
\s1 The execution of Yohan-the-immerser
\r (Mat. 14:1-12; Luke 9:7-9)
\p
\v 14 \add Yeshua\add* was \add now becoming well known\add* and King Herod \add Antipas\add* heard about him. \add He thought that maybe\add* Yohan-the-immerser had \add come back to life\add* and that’s why \add Yeshua\add* \add was able to do\add* miracles.\x + \xo 6:14-15: \xt Mat 16:14; Mrk 8:28; Luk 9:19.\x*
\p
\v 15 But others \add thought it was\add* \add the promised return of\add* Eliyah \add (Elijah)\add*, and \add yet\add* others \add thought he must be\add* \add another\add* prophet like the ones \add in the Scriptures\add*.
\p
\v 16 But hearing \add about Yeshua\add*, Herod was \add telling people\add*, “That Yohan who I \add had\add* beheaded, \add he must have come back to life\add*!”
\v 17 Because Herod himself had had Yohan arrested and \add put\add* in prison because he had married Herodias, his brother Philip’s wife.\x + \xo 6:17-18: \xt Luk 3:19-20.\x*
\v 18 Yohan had been telling \add the king\add* that it was wrong for him to \add marry\add* his brother’s wife,
\v 19 and \add so\add* Herodias resented him \add enough to\add* want to kill him, but \add Herod\add* hadn’t be able to
\v 20 because he respected Yohan as a righteous and holy \add teacher\add* and \add so\add* \add he kept him alive\add*. He \add often\add* enjoyed hearing from \add Yohan\add* and \add thought a lot\add* \add about what he said\add*.
\p
\v 21 \add However,\add* an opportunity came at Herod’s birthday celebration, when he \add threw a feast\add* for his \add top officials\add* and generals, and for the leaders in Galilee.
\v 22 The daughter of Herodias came in and danced \add for them\add*, and the king and \add his party guests all enjoyed it\add*. \add So\add* the king said to the young woman, “\add Just\add* ask me \add for anything\add* and I’ll give it to you.”
\v 23 Then he promised her, “\add Even if you asked for\add* half of my kingdom, I’d give it to you!”
\p
\v 24 \add So\add* she went out and \add asked\add* her mother \add what she should ask for\add*. \add Her mother\add* told \add her that she should ask for\add* Yohan’s head.
\p
\v 25 \add So\add* going back into \add the party\add*, she said seriously to the king, “I would like the head of Yohan-the-immerser \add right now\add* on a plate!”
\p
\v 26 The king \add was really upset\add* \add with that\add*, \add but\add* because \add he’d made loud promises in front of all his guests\add*, he \add couldn’t say no\add*.
\v 27 \add So\add* \add he\add* \add ordered\add* an executioner \add to go out and return with\add* Yohan’s head. He \add went off and\add* beheaded him in the prison
\v 28 and brought \add the\add* head \add into the party\add* on a plate and gave it to the girl, and \add she\add* gave it to her mother.
\v 29 \add When\add* Yohan’s followers heard \add about that\add*, they came and fetched his \add body\add* and \add put\add* it into a tomb.
\s1 Yeshua feeds 5,000 people
\r (Yhn 6:1-14; Mat. 14:13-21; Luke 9:10-17)
\p
\v 30 \add Now\add* the apprentices \add who had been sent out,\add* \add returned\add* back to \nd Yeshua\nd*, and they reported \add back\add* everything they had done and \add what they had been teaching\add* \add people\add*.
\v 31 Then he told them \add to head to\add* a quiet place \add with him to take a break\add*. \add That was\add* because there were \add so many people always\add* coming and going, and \add sometimes\add* they didn’t even have \add a chance\add* to eat.
\v 32 \add So\add* they went away by boat to a quiet place by themselves.
\p
\v 33 \add But\add* \add people from towns all around\add* had seen them going and recognised \add who they were, so\add* they ran \add around the lake\add* on foot and \add got there\add* ahead of them.
\v 34 \add So\add* when \add Yeshua\add* got out \add of the boat\add*, he saw a big crowd and \add felt sorry\add* for them because they \add seemed like\add* sheep without a shepherd, \add so\add* he started teaching them many things.\x + \xo 6:34: \xt Num 27:17; 1Ki 22:17; 2Ch 18:16; Eze 34:5; Mat 9:36.\x*
\v 35 Then, when it was already getting late, his apprentices came up and said, “This place is \add pretty isolated\add* and it’s already late.
\v 36 Send \add these people\add* off \add now\add* so they can go to the \add nearby\add* settlements and villages and buy themselves something to eat.”
\p
\v 37 But \add Yeshua\add* answered them, “\wj You give them something to eat.\wj*”
\p They \add complained\add*, “\add If we go\add*, should we buy \add several months wages worth of bread\add* so we can give \add all of\add* them something to eat?”
\p
\v 38 \add But\add* he asked, “\wj Go \+add and find out\+add* how \+add much bread\+add* you all have.\wj*”
\p \add When they’d found out\add* they said, “Five \add buns\add* and two fish.”
\p
\v 39 \add Yeshua\add* \add told\add* all the people to \add line up\add* in rows \add and\add* sit down on the green grass.
\v 40 \add So\add* they sat down in rows of \add fifty to a hundred\add*.
\v 41 Then \add holding\add* the five buns and two fish, he looked up to the sky and blessed \add God\add*, and then broke up the buns and gave \add pieces\add* to his apprentices so they could \add do likewise\add* to \add the people\add*, and he \add also\add* divided up the two fish,
\v 42 and all \add the people\add* ate and were satisfied.
\v 43 Then they collected the leftover pieces \add of bread\add*, filling twelve baskets, \add as well as the leftover pieces\add* of fish.
\v 44 The \add headcount of\add* men who ate \add that evening\add* was five thousand.
\s1 Yeshua walks on the lake surface
\r (Yhn 6:15-21; Mat. 14:22-33)
\p
\v 45 \add Straight after that,\add* Yeshua \add sent\add* his apprentices \add off\add* in the boat to go \add across\add* to Bethsaida while he sent the crowd \add home\add*.
\v 46 Then he said \add goodbye\add* to \add everyone\add*, \add and\add* \add climbed\add* the hill to pray.
\v 47 \add Later in the night,\add* the boat was out in the middle of the lake, and only \add Yeshua\add* remained back there on the land.
\v 48 He saw them \add struggling\add* to row \add against\add* the wind, and \add some time after 3am\add* he came \add out\add* to them, walking on the lake \add surface\add* \add as if to walk\add* past them.
\v 49 \add But\add* they had seen \add someone\add* walking on the lake \add and thought\add* it was a ghost, and they yelled out
\v 50 because they had all seen it and were \add terrified\add*.
\p \add But\add* \add Yeshua\add* immediately spoke to them and told them, “\wj \+add Hey,\+add* \+add take heart\+add*, \+add it’s me\+add*, don’t be afraid!\wj*”
\v 51 Then he \add climbed\add* up into the boat and the wind died down. They were all \add totally astounded\add*
\v 52 because they hadn’t understood \add the implications of him feeding all the people\add* because their \add minds were closed\add*.
\s1 Yeshua heals many people around Gennesaret
\r (Mat. 14:34-36)
\p
\v 53 \add After completing their crossing\add*, they anchored at Gennesaret.
\v 54 \add When\add* they got out of the boat, \add again\add* \add people\add* recognised \add Yeshua\add*
\v 55 and ran around that \add region\add*, \add so next minute\add* \add people\add* began \add bringing all of\add* those who were sick, carrying them on pallets to wherever they heard that \add Yeshua\add* was.
\v 56 And whenever Yeshua entered villages or cities or fields or marketplaces, they laid the sick down there and were imploring him that they might even just touch the edge of his robe, and anyone who did so was healed.
\c 7
\s1 Yeshua suggests obeying God, not tradition
\r (Mat. 15:1-9)
\p
\v 1 Some from the Pharisee party and some of the law teachers had come from Yerushalem to observe Yeshua
\v 2 and noticed that some of his followers were eating their food without following their religious customs, i.e., without washing their hands.
\v 3 (The Pharisees and, in fact, all the Jewish religious leaders do not eat without first ceremonially washing their hands as taught by their elders over the generations.
\v 4 They won’t eat at the market if they can’t do this first, and have many other regulations about ceremonial washing of cups and jugs and copper kettles.)
\p
\v 5 And the Pharisees and religious teachers asked Yeshua, “How come your followers don’t follow our Jewish traditions, but eat without the ceremonial washing of hands?”
\p
\v 6 And he answered, “\wj Yeshayah got it exactly right when he expressed God’s thoughts about you hypocrites and wrote:\wj*\x + \xo 7:6-7: \xt Isa 29:13 (LXX).\x*
\q1 \wj ‘These people honour me with their lips,\wj*
\q2 \wj but their real desires are far away from mine.\wj*
\q1
\v 7 \wj Their worship of me is worthless,\wj*
\q2 \wj teaching man-made rules as if they are from God.’\wj*
\p
\v 8 \wj You’ve ignored God’s commands instead accepting human traditions.\wj*”
\p
\v 9 And Yeshua continued, “\wj You all simply dismiss God’s commands in favour of your own customs.\wj*
\v 10 \wj Mosheh teaches us,\wj*
\q1 \wj ‘Honour your parents,’\wj*\x + \xo 7:10: \xt Exo 20:12; Deu 5:16.\x*
\p \wj and\wj*
\q1 \wj ‘Anyone dishonestly slandering their parents deserves the death penalty.’\wj*\x + \xo 7:10: \xt Exo 21:17; Lev 20:9.\x*
\m
\v 11 \wj But when you all encourage people to say, ‘Whatever I had that may have helped you guys has been given to God’,\wj*
\v 12 \wj you are actively discouraging them from helping their parents—\wj*
\v 13 \wj overriding God’s voice for your very convenient teaching that you disseminate instead. And that’s not the only example!\wj*”
\s1 Evil comes from inside a person
\r (Mat. 15:10-20)
\p
\v 14 Yeshua called out to the crowd, “\wj Everyone listen to me carefully:\wj*
\v 15 \wj Nothing that enters your mouth makes you guilty in God’s eyes. It’s what comes \+em out\+em* of you that demonstrates your culpability.\wj*”
\p
\v 16 ◘
\v 17 When Yeshua came back inside, his apprentices asked him what he meant.
\v 18 But he scolded them, “\wj Can’t you understand something so simple! Can’t you see that anything from the outside that enters your body doesn’t make you guilty in God’s sight,\wj*
\v 19 \wj because it doesn’t enter your mind but merely goes into your stomach and ends up in the toilet.\wj*” (By saying this he clearly showed that is was ok for Jews to eat any food.)
\p
\v 20 He continued, “\wj It’s what comes out of you that makes you guilty in God’s eyes.\wj*
\v 21-22 \wj That’s because evil thoughts, sexual immorality, theft, murder, adultery, greed, wickedness, deceit, arrogance, lust, slander, pride, and foolishness all come from \+em inside\+em* people’s hearts \+add and minds\+add*.\wj*
\v 23 \wj So it’s the evil that’s inside a person and comes out that makes them guilty in God’s eyes.\wj*”
\s1 The bold faith of a non-Jewish woman
\r (Mat. 15:21-28)
\p
\v 24 Yeshua left that place and went \add up north\add* into the regions of Tyre and Tsidon. He tried to enter a house without being noticed but didn’t succeed,
\v 25 and straight away a woman who had heard about him turned up. She knelt in front of him because she had a little daughter who was demon-possessed.
\v 26 The woman was Greek from Phoenicia in Syria and she begged Yeshua to command the demon to leave her daughter.
\v 27 But he replied, “\wj Let the \+add Jewish\+add* children be treated first, because it’s not good to take the children’s food and throw it to the dogs.\wj*”
\p
\v 28 And she responded, “Yes, \nd master\nd*, but the puppies under the table \em do\em* get to eat the little crumbs that the children drop.”
\p
\v 29 And he answered, “\wj Go home now. As a result of how you just spoke, the demon has already left your daughter.\wj*”
\p
\v 30 And when she got home, the demon had indeed gone and her little girl was lying quietly on her bed.
\s1 Yeshua heals a deaf man who couldn’t speak
\p
\v 31 Yeshua left the Tyre region, passed through Tsidon, and came down the \add eastern\add* side of the Lake of Galilee to the Decapolis region.
\v 32 The people there brought a man to him who couldn’t hear or speak, and asked Yeshua to heal him.
\v 33 So Yeshua took the man back away from the crowd, put his fingers in the man’s ears, then he spat and touched the man’s mouth.
\v 34 Looking up to the sky, he sighed and said, “\wj Open up!\wj*”
\p
\v 35 Then the man could hear, and his tongue was released and he began to speak properly.
\v 36 The Yeshua advised that they shouldn’t tell anyone, but the more he said that, the more they seemed to spread it around.
\v 37 The crowds were totally amazed, saying, “He can do anything—he makes the deaf able to hear, and enables the ones who couldn’t speak.”
\c 8
\s1 Yeshua feeds 4,000 people
\r (Mat. 15:32-39)
\p
\v 1 Another time there was a large crowd again with nothing to eat. Yeshua called his apprentices and told them,
\v 2 “\wj I feel sorry for all these people because they’ve already been with us for three days and don’t have anything to eat.\wj*
\v 3 \wj I can’t send them home because they’re too weak to walk, and some have a long way to go.\wj*”
\p
\v 4 His apprentices queried, “Where could anyone find enough food out here in the wilderness?”
\p
\v 5 He asked, “\wj How much bread have you all got?\wj*”
\p “Seven buns,” they answered.
\p
\v 6 So Yeshua instructed the crowd to sit down on the ground. Then he took the seven buns, gave thanks and broke them and gave pieces to his apprentices to then distribute to the crowd.
\v 7 They also had a few small cooked fish, so he blessed them too and got them to distribute them as well.
\v 8 After all the people had eaten and were full, they collected seven basketfuls of leftovers.
\v 9 There were some four thousand people there that day, and Yeshua sent them home after they’d eaten.
\v 10 And he and his apprentices climbed into the boat and headed for the Dalmanutha region.
\s1 The religious leaders want to see a miracle
\r (Mat. 16:1-4)
\p
\v 11 Some men from the Pharisee party came out to Yeshua and started arguing with him. They were checking him out and wanted to see a miracle.\x + \xo 8:11: \xt Mat 12:38; Luk 11:16.\x*
\v 12 Yeshua grieved inside and asked, “\wj Why do you people need to see a miracle before you’ll believe me? Actually, no miracle will be done for this generation.\wj*”\x + \xo 8:12: \xt Mat 12:39; Luk 11:29.\x*
\p
\v 13 So Yeshua left in the boat again and crossed to the other side of the lake.
\s1 The apprentices miss the significance of what’s happening
\r (Mat. 16:5-12)
\p
\v 14 But Yeshua’s apprentices forgot to take any food other than one bun that someone in the boat had.
\v 15 He told them, “\wj Watch out for the yeast of the Pharisees and of King Herod.\wj*”\x + \xo 8:15: \xt Luk 12:1.\x*
\p
\v 16 They asked each other what it meant—was it because they forgot food?
\p
\v 17 But \nd Yeshua\nd* knew what they were saying and asked, “\wj Why are you all discussing about not having food? Can’t you figure it out? Or are your minds completely closed?\wj*
\v 18 \wj You all have eyes but can’t seem to see and have ears but can’t seem to listen. Have you already forgotten what just happened?\wj*\x + \xo 8:18: \xt Jer 5:21; Eze 12:2; Mrk 4:12.\x*
\v 19 \wj When I broke up the five buns to feed five thousand people, how many basketfuls of leftovers did you collect?\wj*”
\p “Twelve,” they answered.
\p
\v 20 “And when we fed four thousand with seven buns, how many full baskets of leftovers then?”
\p “Seven,” they answered.
\p
\v 21 Yeshua asked, “\wj How can you all not understand what’s going on right in front of you!\wj*”
\s1 Yeshua heals a blind man near Bethsaida
\p
\v 22 As they entered Bethsaida, the people brought a blind man to Yeshua and asked him to touch him.
\v 23 So Yeshua took the man’s hand and led him out of the village. Then he spat in his eyes, placed his hands on him, and asked him, “\wj What can you see?\wj*”
\p
\v 24 The man looked around and answered, “I can see the people, but they just look like trees.”
\v 25 Yeshua touched the man’s eyes again and then he could see clearly—he was now completely better.
\v 26 Then Yeshua told him not to go back into the village but to go straight home.
\s1 Yeshua asks who they think he is
\r (Mat. 16:13-20; Luke 9:18-21)
\p
\v 27 Then \nd Yeshua\nd* and his followers moved around the various villages of Caesaria Philippi. On the way, he asked them, “\wj Who does everyone think I am?\wj*”
\p
\v 28 They said, “Some say Yohan-the-immerser, some say Eliyah, and some say one of the prophets.”\x + \xo 8:28: \xt Mrk 6:14-15; Luk 9:7-8.\x*
\p
\v 29 Then he asked, “\wj And what do you all think?\wj*”
\p Peter said, “You are the promised one sent by God.”\x + \xo 8:29: \xt Yhn 6:68-69.\x*
\p
\v 30 Then Yeshua warned them not to tell others about him.
\s1 Yeshua’s radical challenge to his would-be followers
\r (Mat. 16:21-28; Luke 9:22-27)
\p
\v 31 Then Yeshua began teaching his followers that humanity’s child would need to suffer a lot, to be rejected by the religious leaders, and so to be put to death but to come back to life after three days.
\v 32 He taught this very clearly, but Peter took him aside and told him off.
\v 33 Yeshua turned back and looked at the others, then reprimanded Peter, “\wj Stand back, Satan! you’re applying human values instead of considering God’s plan.\wj*”
\p
\v 34 Calling out to them and to the crowd, he said, “\wj Anyone who wants to follow me must first put aside their own desires and then follow me—even through very difficult times.\wj*\x + \xo 8:34: \xt Mat 10:38; Luk 14:27.\x*
\v 35 \wj Anyone who worries about saving their own life will lose it, but anyone who gives their life due to following me and my teaching will save their life.\wj*\x + \xo 8:35: \xt Yhn 12:25; Mat 10:39; Luk 17:33.\x*
\v 36 \wj Because it doesn’t benefit a person if they gain everything in the world but lose their soul.\wj*
\v 37 \wj What can someone offer in order to buy a home for their soul?\wj*
\v 38 \wj Yes, anyone who is ashamed of me and my message in front of this adulterous and sinful generation, humanity’s child will likewise be ashamed of them when he comes clearly displaying God’s greatness and bringing the holy messengers.\wj*”
\c 9
\p
\v 1 And he continued, “I can assure you that some of the ones standing here won’t die until they have seen God’s powerful kingdom arrive.”
\s1 Mosheh and Eliyah speak with Yeshua
\r (Mat. 17:1-13; Luke 9:28-36)
\p
\v 2 Six days later \nd Yeshua\nd* took Peter and Yacob and Yohan, and just the four of them went up onto a high hill. Then he was changed right in front of their eyes\x + \xo 9:2-7: \xt 2Pe 1:17-18.\x*
\v 3 and his clothes turned incredibly shining white—whiter than any bleach could make them.
\v 4 And Eliyah and Mosheh appeared beside \nd Yeshua\nd* and started talking with him.
\v 5 Peter said to \nd Yeshua\nd*, “Teacher, it’s good that we’re here. Maybe we could make three shelters up here: one for you and one for Mosheh and one for Eliyah?”
\v 6 He said that because they were terrified and didn’t know what to say.
\p
\v 7 Then a cloud came over the sun and a voice came out of the cloud saying, “This is my dear son. Listen to him.”\x + \xo 9:7: \xt Mat 3:17; Mrk 1:11; Luk 3:22.\x*
\v 8 When they looked around, they couldn’t see anyone else there any more, except for \nd Yeshua\nd* himself.
\p
\v 9 As they descended again, Yeshua told them to tell no one what had happened up there until after humanity’s child came back to life.
\p
\v 10 They remembered that, although between all three of them they couldn’t understand the bit about coming back to life.
\v 11 They asked Yeshua, “Isn’t it written that Eliyah will be the first to return?”\x + \xo 9:11: \xt Mal 4:5; Mat 11:14.\x*
\p
\v 12 He answered, “Yes, Eliyah is indeed the one to come first to start the restoration. But isn’t it also written that humanity’s child will suffer a lot and be scorned?
\v 13 You see, Eliyah has come already, and they treated him just however they liked, just like it was written that it would happen.”
\s1 Yeshua drives out a stubborn demon
\r (Mat. 17:14-21; Luke 9:37-43a)
\p
\v 14 When they got back to \add his other\add* followers, Yeshua saw that they were surrounded by a large crowd and the religious teachers were debating with them.
\v 15 The crowd were very surprised when they noticed Yeshua and came running to greet him.
\v 16 He asked them, “\wj What were you all talking about?\wj*”
\p
\v 17 One of the people answered, “Teacher, I brought my son here—he has a demon that prevents him from speaking.
\v 18 Whenever it takes hold of him, it attacks him and he starts foaming at the mouth and grating his teeth and his joints lock up. I tried to get your followers to command it to leave but they couldn’t.”
\p
\v 19 Yeshua replied, “\wj You are a faithless generation. How much longer will I have to be here? How much longer can I tolerate you all? Bring him here.\wj*”
\v 20 So they brought the boy to him. When he saw Yeshua, the demon immediately caused him to have convulsions and fall on the ground—rolling over and foaming at the mouth.
\p
\v 21 Yeshua asked his father, “\wj How long has this been happening?\wj*”
\p “Since he was small,” he replied,
\v 22 “and often it throws him into the fire or into water to try to destroy him. But if you feel sorry for us and can do it, please help us.”
\p
\v 23 \nd Yeshua\nd* asked him, “\wj If I can? A person who believes can do anything.\wj*”
\p
\v 24 The boys father cried out, “I do believe—help me to fight unbelief.”
\p
\v 25 Then \nd Yeshua\nd* noticing that a big crowd was quickly forming, scolded the evil spirit, “\wj You spirit who causes muteness and deafness. Leave this boy now and never return to him.\wj*”
\p
\v 26 There was yelling and strong convulsions, and then the demon left him and the boy lay still as if dead, and many people thought he was.
\v 27 But \nd Yeshua\nd* took his hand and lifted it, and the boy got up.
\p
\v 28 When Yeshua and his apprentices had gone into the house by themselves, they asked him, “How come we couldn’t drive it out?”
\p
\v 29 He replied, “\wj This kind will only leave after prayer and fasting.\wj*”
\s1 Yeshua talks again about being executed
\r (Mat. 17:22-23; Luke 9:43b-45)
\p
\v 30 Then they left that place and were moving around Galilee, because Yeshua didn’t want people to know where he was
\v 31 while he was teaching his apprentices. He told them, “\wj Humanity’s child will be arrested and executed, but after three days he’ll come back to life.\wj*”
\p
\v 32 But they didn’t know what that meant, and they were too scared to ask him.
\s1 How to be the best
\r (Mat. 18:1-5; Luke 9:46-48)
\p
\v 33 Then they went to Capernaum and when they arrived at the house, Yeshua asked them what they were discussing on the way.
\p
\v 34 But they didn’t reply because while they were walking, they had been talking about which of them is the best.\x + \xo 9:34: \xt Luk 22:24.\x*
\v 35 So Yeshua sat down and called the twelve of them and explained, “\wj Anyone who wants to be at the top, has to be at the bottom and help all the others.\wj*”\x + \xo 9:35: \xt Mat 20:26-27; 23:11; Mrk 10:43-44; Luk 22:26.\x*
\v 36 Then Yeshua called a young boy and stood him in the middle of them, and giving him a hug said,
\v 37 “\wj Whoever welcomes young children on my behalf, welcomes me, and anyone who welcomes me is not actually welcoming me, but welcoming the one who sent me here.\wj*”\x + \xo 9:37: \xt Yhn 13:20; Mat 10:40; Luk 10:16.\x*
\s1 Others are also serving Yeshua
\r (Luke 9:49-50)
\p
\v 38 Yohan checked with Yeshua, “Teacher, we saw someone using your authority to command demons to leave, but we told him off because he wasn’t one of us.”
\p
\v 39 \nd Yeshua\nd* responded, “\wj No, don’t stop him because anyone who does a miracle using my name won’t just quickly turn around and slander me.\wj*
\v 40 \wj Anyone who’s not against us is for us.\wj*\x + \xo 9:40: \xt Mat 12:30; Luk 11:23.\x*
\v 41 \wj \+nd Anyone\+nd* who gives you water to drink because you are followers of God’s \+nd chosen\+nd* one and who does it because they are working for me, I can assure you that they’ll receive their reward.\wj*\x + \xo 9:41: \xt Mat 10:42.\x*
\s1 Difficult teachings about disobedience
\r (Mat. 18:6-9; Luke 17:1-2)
\p
\v 42 “\wj But anyone who causes a young person to falter from their trust in me, it would be better if they were thrown into the sea with a heavy weight tied around their neck.\wj*
\v 43 \wj If your hand causes you to disobey God, cut it off—it’s better to enter \+add eternal\+add* life being maimed, than to have two hands but to be thrown into the fire that never goes out.\wj*\x + \xo 9:43: \xt Mat 5:30.\x*
\v 44 ◘
\v 45 \wj And if your foot causes you to disobey God, cut it off—it’s better to enter \+add eternal\+add* life being maimed, than to have two feet but to be thrown into hell.\wj*
\v 46 ◘
\v 47 \wj And if your eye causes you to disobey God, gouge it out—it’s better to enter God’s kingdom one-eyed, than to have two eyes and be thrown into hell\wj*\x + \xo 9:47: \xt Mat 5:29.\x*
\v 48 \wj where the worms never die and the fire never goes out.\wj*
\p
\v 49 “\wj Everyone will be salted with fire.\wj*
\p
\v 50 “\wj Now salt is good, but if it were to go bad, what would you season \+add your food\+add* with then? So keep yourselves like salt, and strive to get on well with each other.\wj*”\x + \xo 9:50: \xt Mat 5:13; Luk 14:34-35.\x*
\c 10
\s1 It’s God that joins husband and wife
\r (Mat. 19:1-12; Luke 16:18)
\p
\v 1 Then Yeshua left \add Capernaum\add* and went down to the Yudean area and across the Yordan river. Again the crowds followed him, and he taught them just like he usually did.
\p
\v 2 Some from the Pharisee party approached Yeshua to check him out and asked, “Does the law allow a husband to dismiss his wife?”
\p
\v 3 “\wj Well, what law did Mosheh give to you all?\wj*” he asked.
\p
\v 4 They answered, “Mosheh said that a husband is allowed to write a statement of divorce and dismiss his wife with it.”\x + \xo 10:4: \xt Deu 24:1-4; Mat 5:31.\x*
\p
\v 5 \nd Yeshua\nd* responded, “\wj Yes, he wrote that law because he saw your stubborn desires,\wj*
\v 6 \wj but God made both men and women right at the beginning.\wj*\x + \xo 10:6: \xt Gen 1:27; 5:2.\x*
\v 7 \wj As a result, a man leaves his parents and joins together with his wife\wj*\x + \xo 10:7-8: \xt Gen 2:24.\x*
\v 8 \wj and the couple become a single unit. So there’s no longer two of them, but only one.\wj*
\v 9 \wj So if it’s \+nd God\+nd* who joins them together like that, no person \+add has the authority\+add* to separate them.\wj*”
\p
\v 10 Back in the house again, his apprentices asked about this.
\v 11 And Yeshua told them, “\wj Whoever divorces his wife and marries another person, commits adultery towards his wife.\wj*”\x + \xo 10:11-12: \xt Mat 5:32; 1Cor 7:10-11.\x*
\v 12 \wj And likewise if the wife divorces her husband and marries another person, she is committing adultery towards her husband.\wj*”
\s1 Yeshua encourages childlike faith
\r (Mat. 19:13-15; Luke 18:15-17)
\p
\v 13 The people were bringing small children for Yeshua to touch, but his apprentices scolded them.
\v 14 However, \nd Yeshua\nd* noticed it and was upset, “\wj Just let those kids come. Don’t prevent them because God’s kingdom consists of people like them.\wj*
\v 15 \wj Actually, you can’t even enter God’s kingdom unless you accept it just like a small child does.\wj*”\x + \xo 10:15: \xt Mat 18:3.\x*
\v 16 So he continued hugging the children and \add commanding\add* a good life for them as he placed his hands on each one.
\s1 Leaving behind everything to be saved
\r (Mat. 19:16-30; Luke 18:18-30)
\p
\v 17 One time when Yeshua was starting out on a trip, a man caught up to him and kneeling down in front of him asked, “Good teacher, what do I have to do to inherit eternal life?”
\p
\v 18 He answered, “\wj Why do you call me \+em good\+em*? No person is good—only \+nd God\+nd* is.\wj*
\v 19 \wj You know \+add God’s\+add* commands: don’t murder, don’t commit adultery, don’t steal, don’t lie about others, don’t defraud others, and honour your parents.\wj*”\x + \xo 10:19: a \xt Exo 20:13; Deu 5:17; \xo b \xt Exo 20:14; Deu 5:18; \xo c \xt Exo 20:15; Deu 5:19; \xo d \xt Exo 20:16; Deu 5:20; \xo e \xt Exo 20:12; Deu 5:16.\x*
\p
\v 20 The man responded, “I’ve kept all of those since I was young.”
\p
\v 21 \nd Yeshua\nd* looked up at him with genuine love and said, “\wj There’s one thing still to be done: sell everything you have and give the money to the poor—then you’ll have wealth in heaven. After that, come and follow me.\wj*”
\v 22 But the man grew very sad when he heard this, because he owned many properties.
\p
\v 23 \nd Yeshua\nd* looked around at his followers, “\wj It’s so difficult for wealthy people to enter God’s kingdom.\wj*”
\p
\v 24 (His followers were a bit stunned at his teaching.) \nd Yeshua\nd* continued, “\wj Yes, it’s certainly difficult to enter into God’s kingdom.\wj*
\v 25 \wj It’s easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle than for a rich person to enter God’s kingdom.\wj*”
\p
\v 26 Now they were totally stunned, asking, “How can anyone possibly be saved then?”
\p
\v 27 \nd Yeshua\nd* looked intently at them, “\wj Yes, humanly it’s quite impossible. But with God it’s different—God can do anything!\wj*”
\p
\v 28 Peter said, “We left everything and came and followed you.”
\p
\v 29 He answered, “\wj Yes I can assure you all that anyone who left their home or farm, and left their siblings or parents or children because of me and my teaching,\wj*
\v 30 \wj they’ll be rewarded one hundred times over with homes and farms and siblings and parents and children along with persecution, but gaining eternal life in the next era.\wj*
\v 31 \wj But many at the top now will be at the bottom then, and many at the bottom now will be blessed then.\wj*”\x + \xo 10:31: \xt Mat 20:16; Luk 13:30.\x*
\s1 Yeshua predicts his execution a third time
\r (Mat. 20:17-19; Luke 18:31-34)
\p
\v 32 They were \add heading south\add* on their way to Yerushalem with Yeshua in front, and his followers were a bit surprised and a bit scared. Yeshua took the twelve aside and started telling them what was going to happen to him,
\v 33 “\wj We’re on our way down to Yerushalem and humanity’s child will be turned over to the chief priests and the religious teachers, and they’ll sentence him to die and pass him over to the non-Jews.\wj*
\v 34 \wj Then they’ll ridicule him and spit on him before they beat him and execute him, but he’ll come back to life again in three days time.\wj*”
\s1 How to become respected in God’s kingdom
\r (Mat. 20:20-28)
\p
\v 35 Then Yacob and Yohan, the two sons of Zebedee approached Yeshua and asked, “Teacher, we’d like to ask a favour from you—please don’t disappoint us.”
\p
\v 36 “\wj What do you want from me?\wj*” Yeshua asked.
\p
\v 37 They answered, “Allow us two to sit beside you in heaven—one of us on each side of you.”
\p
\v 38 But \nd Yeshua\nd* replied, “\wj You don’t even understand what you’re asking for. Do you think you could endure the suffering that I have to go through? Or to be enveloped in the darkness that I have to go down into?\wj*”\x + \xo 10:38: \xt Luk 12:50.\x*
\p
\v 39 And they replied, “Sure we can.”
\p “\wj And indeed you all will suffer like me and be enveloped in darkness like I will be,\wj*” responded \nd Yeshua\nd*,
\v 40 “\wj but as for deciding who will sit next to me, that’s not my decision to make because it’s already been decided.\wj*”
\p
\v 41 When the ten other apprentices heard what these two had asked for, they were upset at Yacob and Yohan.
\v 42 But \nd Yeshua\nd* called them over, “\wj Listen, in most countries their rulers boss the people around and their leaders exert their authority over them.\wj*\x + \xo 10:42-43: \xt Luk 22:25-26.\x*
\v 43 \wj But it doesn’t work like that with you—anyone of you who wanted to be honoured must serve the others,\wj*\x + \xo 10:43-44: \xt Mat 23:11; Mrk 9:35; Luk 22:26.\x*
\v 44 \wj and anyone who wants to become the most respected has to \+add work\+add* like a slave does.\wj*
\v 45 \wj Even humanity’s child didn’t come to be served, but to serve others and to give his life as a ransom to set many people free.\wj*”
\s1 Blind Bartimayos gets his miracle
\r (Mat. 20:29-34; Luke 18:35-43)
\p
\v 46 They arrived at Jericho, and then when Yeshua and his followers were leaving again with a large crowd following, they came across blind Bartimayos sitting beside the path begging.
\v 47 When he heard that it was Yeshua from \nd Nazareth\nd* going past, he yelled out, “Yeshua, \add promised\add* descendant of David, help me please.”
\p
\v 48 When several people told him to stop, he just yelled out even louder, “Yeshua, \add promised\add* descendant of David, please help me.”
\p
\v 49 Then \nd Yeshua\nd* stopped and got the people to call the blind man. So they called him, “Hey, be confident! Get up and come because he’s called for you.”
\p
\v 50 So Bartimayos threw off his jacket, jumped up, and was led to \nd Yeshua\nd*.
\p
\v 51 “\wj Now, what can I do for you?\wj*” \nd Yeshua\nd* asked.
\p “My teacher, I’d like to be able to see,” Bartimayos replied.
\p
\v 52 Then Yeshua responded, “\wj Okay, off you go then—your faith has saved you.\wj*”
\p And immediately Bartimayos was able to see, and followed along the path after Yeshua.
\c 11
\s1 Yeshua is treated like a king as he enters Yerushalem
\r (Yhn 12:12-19; Mat. 21:1-11; Luke 19:28-40)
\p
\v 1 As they got close to Yerushalem and were passing Bethphage and Bethany on the Mt. of Olives, Yeshua sent two of his apprentices ahead, telling them,
\v 2 “\wj Go on to the next village and as you enter it, you’ll see a young donkey that hasn’t ever been ridden yet. Untie it and bring it back here.\wj*
\v 3 \wj If anyone asks what you’re doing, tell them that the \+nd master\+nd* needs it and he’ll return it later.\wj*”
\p
\v 4 So they went ahead and found a donkey tied up on the path just outside the door of a house, and so they went to untie it.
\v 5 But some of the people standing around there said, “Hoy! What do you think you’re doing untying that donkey?”
\p
\v 6 So they responded just as \nd Yeshua\nd* had told them, and the people allowed them to proceed.
\v 7 They brought the young donkey to Yeshua, placing their coats over it before Yeshua mounted it.
\v 8 \add As Yeshua rode it into Yerushalem,\add* many people laid their coats on the road, while others lay down leafy branches cut from trees in nearby fields.
\v 9 Some went ahead and others came along behind, shouting, “This is the saviour who comes with the authority and blessing from \nd Yahweh\nd*—\x + \xo 11:9: \xt Psa 118:25-26.\x*
\v 10 the promised ancestor of King David who is blessed as he ushers in his kingdom—the blessed saviour from heaven.”
\p
\v 11 And so Yeshua arrived in Yerushalem and went into the temple and looked all around, but because it was now evening, he went back out to Bethania village with his twelve apprentices.
\s1 Yeshua curses a fig tree
\r (Mat. 21:18-19)
\p
\v 12 The next day after they’d left Bethania, Yeshua was hungry.
\v 13 From a distance he noticed a fig tree that was in leaf so he went over to see if there was any fruit on it, but when he reached it there was only leaves because it wasn’t the right season for figs.
\v 14 So he spoke to the tree, “\wj No one in this age will ever eat fruit from you again.\wj*” (His followers heard him say that.)
\s1 Yeshua upsets the traders inside the temple
\r (Yhn 2:13-22; Mat. 21:12-17; Luke 19:45-48)
\p
\v 15 Then they came back into Yerushalem and on into the temple where Yeshua started driving out the sellers and the buyers that were trading in there. Then he tipped over the tables of the money-changers and the chairs of the ones selling doves,
\v 16 and stopped others from carrying goods through the temple.
\v 17 He said, “\wj Isn’t it written in the Scriptures,\wj*\x + \xo 11:17: a \xt Isa 56:7; \xo b \xt Jer 7:11.\x*
\q1 \wj ‘My house will be known as a house of prayer for all the nations’?\wj*
\m \wj But all of you have turned it into a sanctuary for swindlers.\wj*”
\p
\v 18 When the chief priests and the religious teachers heard this, they started looking for a way that they could destroy him, because they were afraid of him and the way that the crowd marvelled at his teaching.
\p
\v 19 Every evening, Yeshua and his apprentices would go out of the city to stay overnight.
\s1 Yeshua gives a lesson on moving mountains
\r (Mat. 21:20-22)
\p
\v 20 As they entered the city again in the morning, Yeshua’s apprentices noticed that the fig tree was totally dead.
\v 21 Peter remembered what he had said to the tree, and commented, “Teacher, that fig tree that you cursed has withered up.”
\p
\v 22 \nd Yeshua\nd* responded, “\wj Use your faith in \+nd God\+nd*.\wj*
\v 23 \wj I can assure you that if anyone tells a mountain to go into the sea and doesn’t doubt in their mind but believes that what they’re saying will happen, then it will.\wj*\x + \xo 11:23: \xt Mat 17:20; 1Cor 13:2.\x*
\v 24 \wj So I tell you all that anything you ask God for in your prayers, if you believe that you have received it, then you will.\wj*
\v 25 \wj Whenever you are standing praying, if you have something against someone, be sure to forgive them so that your father in heaven can also forgive your shortcomings.\wj*”\x + \xo 11:25-26: \xt Mat 6:14-15.\x*
\s1 The leaders question Yeshua’s authority
\r (Mat. 21:23-27; Luke 20:1-8)
\p
\v 26 ◘
\v 27 They continued on into Yerushalem and on to the temple where they were met by the chief priests and the religious teachers and the elders.
\v 28 They asked Yeshua, “Do you have any authority to do what you did? If so, who gave it to you?”
\p
\v 29 \nd Yeshua\nd* answered, “\wj Okay, I’ll also ask you something and when you answer me I’ll tell you about my authority to do that:\wj*
\v 30 \wj When Yohan \+add preached and\+add* immersed people, did the authority for that come from heaven or from people here?\wj*”
\p
\v 31 They discussed this among themselves, reasoning, “If we say it was from heaven, he’ll ask us why we didn’t believe him.
\v 32 But we can’t say that it was just from people.” (They said that because they were scared of the crowds, knowing that they believed that Yohan was a prophet.)
\v 33 So they answered, “We don’t know.”
\p And \nd Yeshua\nd* also responded, “\wj Well then, I won’t tell you about my authority to do those things either.\wj*”
\c 12
\s1 The parable about the tenants killing the owner’s son
\r (Mat. 21:33-46; Luke 20:9-19)
\p
\v 1 Then he started telling them some parables: “\wj A man planted a vineyard and put a fence around it, dug out a wine-pressing pit, and built a \+add security\+add* tower. Then he rented it out and went off travelling.\wj*\x + \xo 12:1: \xt Isa 5:1-2.\x*
\v 2 \wj At harvest time, he sent a slave to go to the tenant farmers and get his \+add percentage\+add* of the harvest.\wj*
\v 3 \wj But the tenants grabbed the slave and beat him up, then sent him back empty-handed.\wj*
\v 4 \wj The owner sent a second slave, but they insulted this one and struck him on the head.\wj*
\v 5 \wj So the owner sent a third slave and they murdered him. He sent many other slaves—some they just beat up and others they murdered.\wj*
\v 6 \wj But the owner still had his one, dearly loved son, so finally he sent him to go, thinking that they would now be convinced because it was his son.\wj*
\v 7 \wj But the tenant farmers said to each others, ‘Ah ha. This is the one who will inherit this land. If we kill \+em him\+em*, we’ll be the ones to control the estate.’\wj*
\v 8 \wj So they took him and killed him, and threw the body outside the vineyard fence.\wj*
\p
\v 9 “\wj Now, what do you think the owner of the vineyard will do? Yes, he’ll come and eliminate those \+add evil\+add* tenants, and give the contract to others.\wj*
\v 10 \wj Haven’t you all read this in the scriptures:\wj*\x + \xo 12:10-11: \xt Psa 118:22-23.\x*
\q1 \wj ‘the stone that the builders rejected\wj*
\q1 \wj became the \+add important\+add* cornerstone\wj*
\q1
\v 11 \wj that was given by \+nd Yahweh\+nd*,\wj*
\q1 \wj and we think it’s incredible.’?\wj*”
\p
\v 12 And those leaders tried to arrest him because they knew that it was them that the parable was addressed to, but they couldn’t do it because \add Yeshua had the favour\add* of the crowd, so they just left the area.
\s1 Yeshua answers about payment of taxes
\r (Mat. 22:15-22; Luke 20:20-26)
\p
\v 13 Then the religious leaders sent some members of the Pharisee party and some of the \add political\add* supporters of King Herod to try to catch Yeshua out.
\v 14 So they \add flattered\add* him, “Teacher, we know that you are honest even if it upsets others, and that you teach the truth about \nd God\nd* without caring about people’s opinions. Is it legal \add for us Jews\add* to pay the poll tax to the Roman emperor or not? How should we handle this?”
\p
\v 15 But knowing their hypocrisy, Yeshua responded to them, “\wj Why are you trying to test me? Bring me a coin so we can determine the proper answer.\wj*”
\p
\v 16 When they brought him a coin, he asked, “\wj Whose image and inscription are on this coin?\wj*”
\p And they said, “The emperor’s.”
\p
\v 17 “\wj Well then,\wj*” said \nd Yeshua\nd*, “\wj give to the emperor whatever is due him, but give God what is owed to God.\wj*”
\p And they had to admit that that was an outstanding answer.
\s1 Yeshua explains that those who’ve died are alive
\r (Mat. 22:23-33; Luke 20:27-40)
\p
\v 18 Another time, members of the Sadducee sect came to Yeshua and asked,\x + \xo 12:18: \xt Acts 23:8.\x*
\v 19 “Teacher, Mosheh wrote that if a man’s married brother should die before his wife without having any children, then his brother should marry her to produce descendants in his brother’s name.\x + \xo 12:19: \xt Deu 25:5.\x*
\v 20 Now what if there were seven brothers and the first one got married and died childless.
\v 21 So then the second brother took her in, but again died childless, and then the third.
\v 22 Eventually all seven brothers died childless, and finally the woman also died.
\v 23 Now, at the resurrection of the dead, which brother will she be the wife of, because at some point, she was married to each of the seven of them?”
\p
\v 24 So \nd Yeshua\nd* answered, “\wj Actually you’re all quite wrong, because you ignore the scriptures and also because you haven’t experienced God’s power.\wj*
\v 25 \wj When the dead do come back to life, they won’t be getting engaged or married, but will be like the messengers in heaven.\wj*
\v 26 \wj And as for the state of those who’ve died, didn’t you read in the scriptures about Mosheh when \+nd God\+nd* spoke to him in front of the \+add burning\+add* thorn bush and said, ‘I \+em am\+em* the god of Abraham and the god of Isaac and the god of Yacob’?\wj*\x + \xo 12:26: \xt Exo 3:6.\x*
\v 27 \wj So \+nd God\+nd* is not the \+nd god\+nd* of the dead, but of the living. You Sadducees are very wrong!\wj*”
\s1 Yeshua answers about genuine obedience to God
\r (Mat. 22:34-40; Luke 10:25-28)
\p
\v 28 Then one of the religious teachers who had heard their question and Yeshua’s excellent answer, came closer and asked, “Which of \add God’s\add* commands is the most important?”
\p
\v 29 \nd Yeshua\nd* answered, “\wj Well, the most important is this: ‘Listen, Yisrael. Our \+nd God Yahweh\+nd* is \+add our\+add* master.\wj*\x + \xo 12:29-30: \xt Deu 6:4-5.\x*
\v 30 \wj You must love \+nd Yahweh God\+nd* with all your heart and with all your soul and with all your mind and with all your strength.’\wj*
\v 31 \wj And the second is: ‘Love your neighbour like \+add you look after\+add* yourself.’ There’s no other commands more important than these.\wj*”\x + \xo 12:31: \xt Lev 19:18.\x*
\p
\v 32 The teacher replied to Yeshua, “Yes, teacher, you’re quite right saying there’s only one God and no other,\x + \xo 12:32: \xt Deu 4:35.\x*
\v 33 and saying that we should love him with all our heart and all our intelligence and all our strength, and to be loving our neighbours like we \add look after\add* ourselves, and all this is more important than all the required temple offerings and sacrifices.”\x + \xo 12:33: \xt Hos 6:6.\x*
\p
\v 34 When Yeshua saw that he’d given such a thoughtful answer, he said, “\wj You’re very close to \+nd God’s\+nd* kingdom!\wj*”\x + \xo 12:28-34: \xt Luk 10:25-28.\x*
\p After that, no one else dared to ask Yeshua any more questions.
\s1 Yeshua asks about the messiah being David’s descendant
\r (Mat. 22:41-46; Luke 20:41-44)
\p
\v 35 Later when \nd Yeshua\nd* was teaching in the temple, he asked the people, “\wj How is it that the teachers here say that the \+nd messiah\+nd* is a descendant of King David?\wj*
\v 36 \wj Because David himself said under the inspiration of the holy \+nd spirit\+nd*,\wj*\x + \xo 12:36: \xt Psa 110:1.\x*
\q1 \wj ‘The \+nd master\+nd* told my \+nd master\+nd*,\wj*
\q2 \wj “Sit there in the position of honour\wj*
\q2 \wj until I can conquer your enemies.” ’\wj*
\m
\v 37 \wj If David calls \+add the messiah\+add* his \+nd master\+nd*, how can \+add the messiah\+add* possibly be \+add David’s\+add* descendant?\wj*”
\p The large crowd was very interested to hear everything that Yeshua had to say.
\s1 Yeshua warns about well-dressed teachers
\r (Mat. 23:1-36; Luke 20:45-47)
\p
\v 38 As Yeshua continued teaching, he said, “\wj Watch out for these religious teachers who like to wear \+add fancy\+add* robes and be greeted in the square,\wj*
\v 39 \wj and have the best seats at meetings and the best places at dinners.\wj*
\v 40 \wj These people trick widows out of their properties, but offer long prayers to cover it up. They will be getting a stricter judgement than others.\wj*”
\s1 The sacrificial donation of a widow
\r (Luke 21:1-4)
\p
\v 41 As he sat down in front of the \add temple\add* treasury, Yeshua watched the crowd as they threw coins into \add the dish\add*, and some rich people were donating quite valuable \add silver\add* coins.
\v 42 Then an impoverished widow came and threw in two small \add copper\add* coins.
\v 43 Yeshua called his followers and told them, “\wj Honestly, that poor widow gave more than all the others.\wj*
\v 44 \wj You see, most people donate from their excess wealth, but this woman even gave \+add some of\+add* the small amount that she needed to live on.\wj*”
\c 13
\s1 Yeshua predicts total destruction of the temple
\r (Mat. 24:1-2; Luke 21:5-6)
\p
\v 1 As they were leaving the temple area, one of Yeshua’s followers noted, “Teacher, look at those \add large, chiselled\add* stones and the \add beautiful\add* buildings \add that they’ve been made into\add*.”
\p
\v 2 But \nd Yeshua\nd* responded, “\wj Yes, incredible buildings, but \+add the time will come\+add* when they’re torn down—not a single \+add piece of\+add* stone will be left sitting in its place.\wj*”
\s1 Yeshua talks about coming hardships
\r (Mat. 24:3-14; Luke 21:7-19)
\p
\v 3 Later when they were over on the Mount of Olives overlooking the temple, Peter and Yacob and Yohan and Andrew were with Yeshua and asked
\v 4 when all this destruction would happen, and what indications there would be that it would be about to happen.
\p
\v 5 So \nd Yeshua\nd* told them, “\wj Be careful so you aren’t misled by others.\wj*
\v 6 \wj Plenty of people will say that they’re coming \+add because they were sent by me\+add*, or that they \+em are\+em* me, and they’ll mislead many others.\wj*
\v 7 \wj And whenever you hear about wars or about battles about to begin, don’t be worried—those things will certainly happen, but there’s more to follow.\wj*
\v 8 \wj Countries will take up arms against other countries, and kingdoms against other kingdoms. There’ll be earthquakes in \+add many\+add* places and there’ll be famines, but those will be just like the beginning of birth pains.\wj*
\p
\v 9 “\wj So be careful. You’ll be turned over to \+add local\+add* councils \+add for questioning\+add*, will be whipped in the Jewish meeting halls, and will be stood in front of governors and kings because of following me. But you’ll be able to present your life stories to them\wj*\x + \xo 13:9-11: \xt Mat 10:17-20; Luk 12:11-12.\x*
\v 10 \wj and it’s right that the good news first\wj*\f + \fr 13:10 \ft TD: It’s not clear here what was meant by ‘first’—‘first’ before what?\f* \wj be proclaimed to every people group.\wj*
\v 11 \wj But whenever they bring you along to hand you over, don’t worry in advance about what you’re going to say to defend yourselves, but wait until the hearing proceeds and speak what you are given then, because then it won’t be you speaking, but \+add God’s\+add* \+nd spirit\+nd*.\wj*
\v 12 \wj In those times, a brother will report on his brother to have him executed, and a father report on his child, and children will be encouraged to report on their own parents and then they’ll be executed as a result.\wj*
\v 13 \wj And every one will hate you all because they hate my name, but anyone who endures to the end will be saved.\wj*\x + \xo 13:13: \xt Mat 10:22.\x*
\s1 Yeshua warns about coming horror and deceptions
\r (Mat. 24:15-28; Luke 21:20-24)
\p
\v 14 “\wj But whenever you see the most horrible thing that drives people from God, standing where it shouldn’t be (and you readers should understand this), then anyone in Yudea should flee into the hills\wj*\x + \xo 13:14: \xt Dan 9:27; 11:31; 12:11.\x*
\v 15 \wj and anyone upstairs looking outside shouldn’t come down into the bedrooms to get anything,\wj*\x + \xo 13:15-16: \xt Luk 17:31.\x*
\v 16 \wj and anyone working outside shouldn’t go back inside to get a change of clothes.\wj*
\v 17 \wj It will be extremely difficult for pregnant women and for mothers who are breast-feeding their children in those days.\wj*
\v 18 \wj Just pray that it won’t happen in the winter\wj*
\v 19 \wj because those days will be terrible like nothing that’s ever happened before, all the way back to when \+nd God\+nd* created the world right until the present time, and won’t ever happen again.\wj*\x + \xo 13:19: \xt Dan 12:1; Rev 7:14.\x*
\v 20 \wj And if \+nd Yahweh\+nd* hadn’t shortened that time, no humans would even survive, but he shortened that time so that the chosen ones could be saved.\wj*
\p
\v 21 \wj So don’t believe it if you hear someone say, ‘Look, the \+nd messiah\+nd* has arrived here.’ or ‘There’s the one that God has sent.’\wj*
\v 22 \wj Plenty of fake messiahs and deceiving prophets will certainly come and even do things that humans can’t normally do, so much so that even the chosen ones would defect if that were possible.\wj*
\v 23 \wj So watch out, but I’ve warned you in advance about all of this.\wj*
\s1 The universe collapses as the son arrives
\r (Mat. 24:29-31; Luke 21:25-28)
\p
\v 24 “\wj After those very difficult times, the sun will go dark and the moon won’t shine at night.\wj*\x + \xo 13:24: a \xt Isa 13:10; Yoel 2:10,31; 3:15; Rev 6:12; \xo b \xt Isa 13:10; Eze 32:7.\x*
\v 25 \wj The stars will fall down out of the sky and the powers in the heavens will be shaken up.\wj*\x + \xo 13:25: a \xt Isa 34:4; Rev 6:13; \xo b \xt Yoel 2:10.\x*
\v 26 \wj Then the people will see humanity’s child in the clouds coming with incredible power and brightness.\wj*\x + \xo 13:26: \xt Dan 7:13; Rev 1:7.\x*
\v 27 \wj He’ll send the messengers out to collect all the chosen people from the four corners of the world, from everywhere between the earth and the sky.\wj*
\s1 Watch out for the change in season
\r (Mat. 24:32-35; Luke 21:29-33)
\p
\v 28 “\wj So learn a lesson from fig trees—whenever the branches green up and start sprouting leaves and then everyone knows that summer is close.\wj*
\v 29 \wj So too whenever you see all these things happening, you know that the end \+add of this current world\+add* is close.\wj*
\v 30 \wj I can assure you that the generation of people who see these things won’t die off before these latter things happen.\wj*
\v 31 \wj The sky and the planet might collapse, but my teachings will never become irrelevant.\wj*
\s1 Stay alert for the coming of the master
\r (Mat. 24:36-44)
\p
\v 32 “\wj But as for the date or time when all this will happen, only the father knows that—even the son and messengers don’t know.\wj*\x + \xo 13:32: \xt Mat 24:36.\x*
\v 33 \wj So watch out, observe current events, and be praying, because you won’t know exactly when all this will happen.\wj*
\v 34 \wj It can all be compared to a man who was about to leave his house and go on a trip. Before he leaves, he gives certain jobs and responsibilities to his various slaves, and instructs his security guard to stay alert.\wj*\x + \xo 13:34: \xt Luk 12:36-38.\x*
\v 35 \wj So you all watch and stay alert, because you don’t know when the master of the house might return—perhaps one evening or at midnight, or at dawn or maybe mid-morning.\wj*
\v 36 \wj You certainly don’t want to be caught napping when he comes.\wj*
\v 37 \wj And what I’m telling you now, I tell everyone: stay alert and watch.\wj*”
\c 14
\s1 The leaders plan to eliminate Yeshua
\r (Yhn 11:45-53; Mat. 26:1-5; Luke 22:1-2)
\p
\v 1 Now it was two days before the Passover Feast and the \it Flat Bread Celebration\it*, and the chief priests and the religious teachers were trying to figure out how they could have Yeshua set up so they could arrest him and have him executed,\x + \xo 14:1: \xt Exo 12:1-27.\x*
\v 2 but they knew that the Feast wouldn’t be a good time because the people would protest \add in Yeshua’s favour\add*.
\s1 The woman anoints Yeshua for his burial
\r (Yhn 12:1-8; Mat. 26:6-13)
\p
\v 3 Meanwhile, Yeshua was in Bethany at the home of Simon who had previously had leprosy. Yeshua was sitting there when a woman came in with a hand-crafted container of very expensive fragrant oil. Breaking open the sealed flask, she poured the fragrant oil over Yeshua’s head.\x + \xo 14:3: \xt Luk 7:37-38.\x*
\v 4 Immediately some started quietly grumbling about the extravagant waste,
\v 5 then they scolded the woman because a flask of genuine lotion like this could easily fetch almost a year’s wages if it was sold.
\p
\v 6 But \nd Yeshua\nd* spoke up, “\wj What do you think you’re doing abusing her—leave her alone! She’s done something very good for me.\wj*
\v 7 \wj You’ll always have poor people around, and you can help them whenever you want to, but I won’t be around forever.\wj*\x + \xo 14:7: \xt Deu 15:11.\x*
\v 8 \wj She has performed the traditional anointing of my body in advance of my burial.\wj*
\v 9 \wj And I can assure you that wherever the good news is proclaimed around the world, what she has done today will be part of the story and she’ll be remembered for it.\wj*”
\s1 Yudas offers to hand Yeshua over
\r (Mat. 26:14-16; Luke 22:3-6)
\p
\v 10 Then Yudas Iscariot, one of Yeshua’s twelve \add apprentices\add*, visited the chief priests to offer to hand Yeshua over to them.
\v 11 When they heard this, they were very pleased and promised to pay him well, and Yudas asked them about the most convenient way for them to get hold of Yeshua.
\s1 Yeshua’s apprentices prepare the Passover meal
\r (Yhn 13:21-30; Mat. 26:17-25; Luke 22:7-14, 21-23)
\p
\v 12 On the first day of the \it Flat Bread Celebration\it* when the lambs would be slaughtered ready for the special Passover meal, Yeshua’s apprentices asked him, “Where do you want us to get everything ready for our Passover meal?”
\p
\v 13 So Yeshua sent off two of them with these instructions, “\wj Go into town and a man carrying a pitcher of water will meet you there. Just follow him\wj*
\v 14 \wj until he goes into a house. Then find the owner of the house and tell him that the teacher wants to know the location of the guestroom where he and his apprentices can celebrate the Passover meal.\wj*
\v 15 \wj He’ll show you a large, furnished room upstairs—that’s where you can get everything ready.\wj*”
\p
\v 16 So the two of them went off into the town, and everything happened there exactly as Yeshua had told them, and so they prepared the special meal there.
\s1 Yeshua shocks his apprentices at the Passover meal
\r (Yhn 13:21-30; Mat. 26:20-30; Luke 22:14-23; 1 Cor. 11:23-25)
\p
\v 17 As the evening approached, Yeshua went in for dinner with his twelve apprentices.
\v 18 As they were sitting around eating, he told them, “\wj I’ll tell you frankly that one of you eating with me here will hand me over \+add to be arrested\+add*.\wj*”\x + \xo 14:18: \xt Psa 41:9.\x*
\v 19 That upset them, and several of them individually asked, “Surely it couldn’t be me?”
\p
\v 20 Then he continued, “\wj It’s one of you twelve—the one who dips his bread in the sauce at the same time as me.\wj*
\v 21 \wj Indeed it’s written \+add in the scriptures\+add* that humanity’s child will be arrested, but on the other hand, what a tragedy it’ll be for the one who hands him over—in fact it would be better for that man if he was never born.\wj*”
\p
\v 22 As they carried on eating, he took some bread, blessed \add God\add*, broke it apart and gave a piece to them, saying, “\wj Take this bread—it’s my body.\wj*”
\p
\v 23 Then he picked up a cup, gave thanks \add to God\add*, and passed it around so they could each drink from it,
\v 24 saying, “\wj This is the blood of my contract that will be poured out for many \+add people\+add*.\wj*\x + \xo 14:24: a \xt Exo 24:8; \xo b \xt Jer 31:31-34.\x*
\v 25 \wj I can assure you that I won’t drink anything made from grapes until I drink new juice in God’s kingdom.\wj*”
\p
\v 26 And after they’d sung some songs, they left the town and went across to the Mount of Olives.
\s1 Yeshua predicts Peters denials
\r (Yhn 13:36-38; Mat. 26:31-35; Luke 22:31-34)
\p
\v 27 Then \nd Yeshua\nd* continued his teaching, “\wj All of you will stumble because it’s written in the scriptures: ‘I’ll strike down the shepherd and the sheep will be scattered.’\wj*\x + \xo 14:27: \xt Zech 13:7.\x*
\v 28 \wj But after I come back to life, I’ll go on ahead of you back to Galilee.\wj*”\x + \xo 14:28: \xt Mat 28:16.\x*
\p
\v 29 But Peter contradicted him, “Even if everyone else stumbles, I won’t!”
\p
\v 30 And \nd Yeshua\nd* responded, “\wj I can assure you that you’ll renounce me three times this very night, before the rooster crows early tomorrow morning.\wj*”
\p
\v 31 But Peter argued forcibly, “Even if I had to die with you, I would never disown you!”
\p And all the others said similar things.
\s1 Yeshua, in distress, prays at Gethsemane
\r (Mat. 26:36-46; Luke 22:39-46)
\p
\v 32 They walked along until they came to a piece of land called Gethsemane where Yeshua told his apprentices to sit down while he prayed.
\v 33 He took Peter and Yacob and Yohan further in with him where he stared off in a daze but was obviously very distressed.
\v 34 He told the three of them, “\wj My soul is deathly sad! You guys just stay here and keep alert.\wj*”
\p
\v 35 He went ahead a little way, where he knelt on the ground with his head down and prayed—asking if it was possible to avoid what was ahead of him.
\v 36 But he said, “\wj Father, I know that you can do anything. Let me avoid this suffering, but yes I do know, it doesn’t matter what I want but rather what you’ve \+add planned\+add*.\wj*”
\p
\v 37 Then he went back and found the others sleeping. He asked Peter, “\wj Simon, are you asleep? Couldn’t you stay awake even for a single hour?\wj*
\v 38 \wj You all need to stay alert and be careful not to give into temptation. I know your spirits are enthusiastic on one hand, but it’s still easy to give in to the moment.\wj*”
\p
\v 39 Then he went further in again and prayed as before,
\v 40 but when he returned again, he found them all asleep because they were tired, but they couldn’t really find any excuse to give.
\p
\v 41 The third time that Yeshua returned, he said to them, “\wj Ah, you’re all sleeping again and having a good rest. It’s well earned but the time has come for humanity’s child to be handed over to evil men.\wj*
\v 42 \wj Come on—get up—we need to go! Look, the one who’ll hand me over is on his way.\wj*”
\s1 Yeshua is arrested in the night
\r (Yhn 18:3-12; Mat. 26:47-56; Luke 22:47-53)
\p
\v 43 Even while Yeshua was still speaking, Yudas reached him, followed by a crowd from the chief priests, the religious teachers, and the elders—many armed with swords and clubs.
\v 44 Yudas had prearranged a signal with them that anyone who he greeted with a kiss, they should grab that person and lead him away under guard.
\p
\v 45 So he walked right up to Yeshua and said, “My honoured teacher,” and greeted him with a kiss.
\v 46 Then the others with him grabbed Yeshua and secured him.
\v 47 But one of the ones with Yeshua that was beside him, drew his sword and struck a slave of the chief priest, cutting off the man’s ear.
\v 48 \nd Yeshua\nd* spoke to the crowd, “\wj Did you all bring swords and clubs to try to capture me like you’d chase down a thief?\wj*
\v 49 \wj I was standing there teaching in the temple every day and you could have arrested me, but it’s happened this way to ensure that the what’s written in scriptures will be fulfilled.\wj*”\x + \xo 14:49: \xt Luk 19:47; 21:37.\x*
\p
\v 50 Meanwhile, the others who’d been with Yeshua had taken off.
\v 51 One young fellow was wearing a single linen robe, and when the crowd tried to grab him,
\v 52 it came off \add in the struggle\add* and he ran away stark naked.
\s1 The mock trial where Yeshua is sentenced to death
\r (Yhn 18:13-14,19-24; Mat. 26:57-68; Luke 22:54-55,63-71)
\p
\v 53 The guards led \nd Yeshua\nd* away to the chief priest’s residence, and all the chief priests and the elders and the religious teachers gathered there also.
\v 54 Peter followed along from a distance and went in as far as the courtyard of the residence where he joined the servants who were sitting around the fire keeping warm.
\v 55 Inside, the chief priests and all the council were trying to establish a case against \nd Yeshua\nd* so he could be sentenced to death, but they weren’t coming up with anything.
\v 56 Several people were making up charges against him, but none of the accusers’ stories agreed with each other.
\p
\v 57 Eventually some of them stood up and offered this false charge:
\v 58 “We heard him say that he would demolish this temple made by many labourers, and within three days he’d build another one without any helpers.”\x + \xo 14:58: \xt Yhn 2:19.\x*
\v 59 But even then, they couldn’t get their accusations to align with each other.
\p
\v 60 Nevertheless the chief priest in the middle of the council stood up and asked \nd Yeshua\nd*, “Aren’t you going to defend yourself? These men are accusing you of these things.”
\p
\v 61 But Yeshua didn’t say anything. So the chief priest asked again, “Is it true that you’re the \nd chosen\nd* one that God promised to send—the son of the blessed \add God\add*?”
\p
\v 62 \nd Yeshua\nd* replied, “I am. And you will all see humanity’s child sitting in the honoured position by the powerful God and coming through the clouds in the sky.”\x + \xo 14:62: \xt Dan 7:13.\x*
\p
\v 63 Then the chief priest tore his own robe \add to show his disgust\add* and shouted, “We certainly don’t need any more witnesses now!
\v 64 You yourselves all heard him insult God. What sentence does that deserve?”\x + \xo 14:64: \xt Lev 24:16.\x*
\p So the leaders there agreed that Yeshua needed to be sentenced to death.
\p
\v 65 Then various people started spitting on him, and blindfolding him and beating him saying, “Ha, tell us who that was!” Even the servants joined in to slap him.
\s1 Peter disowns Yeshua
\r (Yhn 18:15-18,25-27; Mat. 26:69-75; Luke 22:56-62)
\p
\v 66 Meanwhile, Peter was down in the courtyard when one of the chief priest’s servant girls came out,
\v 67 and seeing Peter warming himself, she looked more closely and said, “Hey, you were with that \nd Yeshua\nd* from \nd Nazareth\nd*!”
\p
\v 68 But he denied it, “I don’t know him and I don’t know why you think I would!” And he moved away from the fire.
\p
\v 69 But the girl that had seen him said to some of the others standing around, “That guy’s one of them.”
\p
\v 70 But again Peter denied it.
\p A little while later, some of those standing there said, “You must be one of them because you’re from Galilee.”
\p
\v 71 Now Peter started swearing and cursing and said, “I’ve never even met that fellow that you’re all talking about!”
\p
\v 72 Right then the rooster crowed for the second time and Peter remembered now what Yeshua had told him—how he would renounce Yeshua three times before the rooster crowed—and he broke down and cried.
\c 15
\s1 Pilate tries to interrogate Yeshua
\r (Yhn 18:28-38; Mat. 27:1-2,11-14; Luke 23:1-5)
\p
\v 1 First thing in the morning, the chief priests called a meeting with the elders and the religious teachers and all the council, and they ordered that \nd Yeshua\nd* be tied up and taken over to Governor Pilate.
\v 2 There Pilate asked him, “Are you the king of the Jews?”
\p “\wj You said it,\wj*” replied Yeshua.
\p
\v 3 Then the chief priests started hurling accusations against Yeshua,
\v 4 so Pilate asked him again, “Don’t you have anything to say? Listen to all these accusations they’re making against you.”
\p
\v 5 But Pilate was astounded that \nd Yeshua\nd* didn’t answer any further.
\s1 Pilate agrees to execute an innocent man
\r (Yhn 18:39–19:16; Mat. 27:15-26; Luke 23:13-25)
\p
\v 6 Now at time of the Jewish Passover, Pilate would normally pardon one prisoner for them and release him.
\v 7 There was another prisoner at the time named Barabbas who was an activist accused of murder.
\v 8 By now, the crowd had arrived and asked Pilate to release a prisoner as usual at Passover,
\v 9 so he asked them, “Do you want me to release the King of the Jews for you?”
\v 10 (This was because Pilate was well aware that the chief priests had brought Yeshua because they were jealous of his \add popularity\add*.)
\v 11 But the chief priests stirred up the crowd to start saying that it was Barabbas that they wanted released,
\v 12 so Pilate asked again, “Then what should I do with this man that you call the King of the Jews?”
\p
\v 13 The crowd yelled back, “Execute him on a pole.”
\p
\v 14 “What has he even done wrong?” Pilate demanded.
\p But they just yelled louder, “Execute him on a pole!”
\p
\v 15 Now Pilate wanted to placate the crowd, so he had Barabbas released while he had \nd Yeshua\nd* flogged and then handed over to the execution squad.
\s1 The battalion plays with their mock ‘king’
\r (Yhn 19:2-3; Mat. 27:27-31)
\p
\v 16 So the soldiers led Yeshua inside the palace that was the governor’s residence, and called in the rest of the battalion.
\v 17 They \add mocked Yeshua\add* by dressing him in a kingly robe, and after someone twisted some thorns together into a mock crown, they pressed it onto his head,
\v 18 and began saluting him, “Greetings, king of the Jews.”
\v 19 They took turns whacking his head with a stick and spitting on him, while others knelt down in front of him and pretended to be bowing to him.
\v 20 After they’d finished having fun mocking him, they took the purple robe off him, and put his own robe back on him. Then they led him outside towards the execution spot.
\s1 Yeshua is publicly executed along with thieves
\r (Yhn 19:17-27; Mat. 27:32-44; Luke 23:26-43)
\p
\v 21 As they walked out of the city, they came across a man coming in from the country. He was Simon from Cyrene, the father of Alexander and Rufus, and the soldiers forced him to carry the execution pole,\x + \xo 15:21: \xt Rom 16:13.\x*
\v 22 and so they all arrived at Golgotha (which means ‘Place of the skull’).
\v 23 There the soldiers went to give some spiced wine to Yeshua, but he declined it.
\v 24 So they carried out the execution, taking Yeshua’s clothes and drawing straws to see who would get what.\x + \xo 15:24: \xt Psa 22:18.\x*
\v 25 By now it was around 9am, and they affixed Yeshua to the pole.
\v 26 The charge had been written out and attached \add at the top\add*: “The king of the Jews.”
\v 27 That morning, two thieves were also executed there—one on each side of Yeshua.
\p
\v 28 ◘
\v 29 As people passed by on the path, they insulted Yeshua, shaking their heads and shouting, “Hoy there! You who reckoned you could demolish the temple and rebuild it in three days,\x + \xo 15:29: a \xt Psa 22:7; 109:25; \xo b \xt Yhn 2:19; Mrk 14:58.\x*
\v 30 come down from that pole and save yourself.”
\p
\v 31 Similarly the chief priests and the religious teachers laughed together and mocked, “He did miracles for others, but he can’t even save himself.
\v 32 The so-called ‘\nd chosen\nd* one’ or ‘king of Yisrael’. Now, if he’d just come down from there, then we’d be able to believe him.”
\p Even the other two being executed with him were deriding him.
\s1 Yeshua breathes his last
\r (Yhn 19:28-30; Mat. 27:45-56; Luke 23:44-49)
\p
\v 33 At noon, it got dark everywhere, until around 3pm,
\v 34 when \nd Yeshua\nd* shouted loudly, “\wj My god, my god, why have you abandoned me?\wj*”\x + \xo 15:34: \xt Psa 22:1.\x*
\p
\v 35 Some of the people standing nearby thought he was calling the prophet Eliyah \add (Elijah)\add*.
\v 36 One of them ran, and soaked a sponge in some sour wine, then put it on a stick and held it up for Yeshua to drink, saying, “Hang on. Let’s see if Eliyah does come to get him down.”\x + \xo 15:36: \xt Psa 69:21.\x*
\p
\v 37 But just then \nd Yeshua\nd* uttered a loud cry and exhaled his last breath.
\p
\v 38 (Back in the temple, the curtain tore down the middle from the top to the bottom.)\x + \xo 15:38: \xt Exo 26:31-33.\x*
\v 39 When Yeshua died, the Roman centurion who had been standing back watching everything said, “This man was definitely God’s son.”
\p
\v 40 Further back, there was also a group of women observing everything. These included Maria from Magdala, Maria the mother of young Yacob and Yoses, and Salome,\x + \xo 15:40-41: \xt Luk 8:2-3.\x*
\v 41 and others who had followed Yeshua around Galilee and helped look after him, along with many others who had come down with him to Yerushalem.
\s1 Yosef boldly lays the body in a burial chamber
\r (Yhn 19:38-42; Mat. 27:57-61; Luke 23:50-56)
\p
\v 42 As evening came, it was now the Preparation Day, i.e., the day before the Rest Day.
\v 43 Yosef (who’d come from the city of Arimathea and one who was waiting for the coming of God’s kingdom), a prominent council member, steeled himself up and went in to see the governor to get permission to transfer \nd Yeshua’s\nd* body.
\v 44 Governor Pilate didn’t think he’d be dead yet, so he called an army commander to find out if Yeshua had indeed died.
\v 45 As soon as he found out, he gave Yosef permission,
\v 46 so Yosef \add and his companions\add* went and bought some linen burial cloth and then, removing Yeshua’s body from the pole, wrapped it in the cloth and placed it in a burial chamber that had been chiselled into a rockface. Then they rolled a rock across the opening.
\v 47 Others were watching and noted where the body had been laid, including Maria from Magdala and Maria who was Yoses’ mother.
\c 16
\s1 The massive shock at the burial chamber
\r (Yhn 20:1-10; Mat. 28:1-8; Luke 24:1-12)
\p
\v 1 After the Rest Day was over, Maria from Magdala and Maria the mother of Yacob and Salome, went and bought some traditional burial spices that evening to take to the burial chamber and place with the body.
\v 2 Then very early on Sunday morning they went to the chamber, arriving just as the sun was rising.
\v 3 But they realised that they didn’t have anyone with them to roll the rock away that was covering the entrance.
\v 4 However, when they looked they saw that the very large rock had already been rolled away.
\v 5 So they went on into the burial chamber and were totally stunned to find a young man dressed in white, sitting in there on the side.
\p
\v 6 He assured them, “Don’t be surprised. I know you’re looking for \nd Yeshua\nd* from \nd Nazareth\nd*, the one who was executed on a pole. But see there where his body was—he’s not there because he came back to life.
\v 7 So you three should go and tell his apprentices, and Peter in particular, that he’s going to go up ahead of them to Galilee and you’ll all see him up there, just as he already told you.”\x + \xo 16:7: \xt Mat 26:32; Mrk 14:28.\x*
\p
\v 8 So they left the chamber and took off running, still stunned and shaking and too scared to stop and to talk to anyone.
\rem It’s not certain whether or not the following longer ending of this document was actually penned by the same author (Markos/Mark), or whether it was appended slightly later. Expert scholars are divided on this, and hence it’s included here for your consideration:
\s1 \add The others don’t believe that Maria saw Yeshua\add*
\r (Yhn 20:11-18; Mat. 28:9-10)
\p
\v 9 \add After coming back to life early on Sunday morning, Yeshua appeared first to Maria from Magdala. (She was the one that he had once commanded seven demons to leave.)\add*
\v 10 \add After leaving him, she went and told his followers who were mourning and weeping at his loss.\add*
\v 11 \add But when she told them that he is alive again and that she’d seen him, they simply didn’t believe her.\add*
\s1 \add Yeshua appears to two of his followers\add*
\r (Luke 24:13-35)
\p
\v 12 \add Later on, Yeshua appeared in a different form to two of his followers on the path as they left Yerushalem and headed out to the countryside,\add*
\v 13 \add but when they hurried back and told the others, no one believed them.\add*
\s1 \add Yeshua appears to his apprentices and instructs them\add*
\r (Yhn 20:19-23; Mat. 28:16-20; Luke 24:36-49; Acts 1:6-8)
\p
\v 14 \add Later again when the eleven apprentices were sitting around eating, Yeshua appeared in the room and scolded them for not believing and for being so stubborn when they hadn’t believed others who had told them that he’d come back from the dead.\add*
\v 15 \add Then he instructed them, “\+wj Travel around all the world\+wj*\add*\x + \xo 16:15: \xt Acts 1:8.\x* \add \+wj and tell everyone the good message.\+wj*\add*
\v 16 \add \+wj Anyone who believes and gets immersed in water will be saved, however, those who refuse to believe will be condemned to death.\+wj*\add*
\v 17 \add \+wj Those who believe will be able to do miracles: they’ll be able to use my authority to command demons to leave and to speak new languages,\+wj*\add*
\v 18 \add \+wj and they’ll be able to pick up snakes. If they drink anything poisonous, it won’t hurt them, and they will place their hands on people and make them better.\+wj*”\add*
\s1 \add Yeshua is lifted up to heaven\add*
\r (Luke 24:50-53; Acts 1:9-11)
\p
\v 19 \add When Yeshua had finished speaking, he was lifted up into heaven and seated in the honoured place\add*\x + \xo 16:19: \xt Acts 1:9-11.\x* \add next to God.\add*
\v 20 \add Then his followers went around preaching everywhere, and God worked through them and so they were able to confirm the truth of their preaching by doing miracles.\add*
